INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$dc234d74$SATQ$, $SATQ$a1e8699f-954c-4f4e-b0ea-95f400636c11$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The recently observed gamma ray burst GRB 230307A lasted for 200 seconds, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> for a burst generated by the merger of neutron stars. Bursts caused by neutron mergers typically last fewer than 2 seconds.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b448cb13-81d7-4cbf-89b8-1ac8a1378b82","content":"<p>a coincidence&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c4a681e7-90a3-4346-9880-4b8810306f46","content":"<p>a reprieve</p>"},{"id":"f30f28bf-28cf-471d-908f-39c7adc555b6","content":"<p>an incident</p>"},{"id":"3cfe237b-f66d-4a0a-9f7d-c487b6b6853a","content":"<p>an oddity</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3cfe237b-f66d-4a0a-9f7d-c487b6b6853a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the duration of gamma ray burst GRB 230307A. In this context, "an oddity" is something that is odd or unusual. The text explains that the burst lasted for 200 seconds and that other bursts generated by neutron mergers have usually lasted fewer than 2 seconds. In other words, the duration of gamma ray burst GRB 230307A was unusual.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text focuses on a difference between the duration of gamma ray burst GRB 230307A and the typical duration of bursts caused by neutron mergers, not "a coincidence," or a point of correspondence, between them; the text indicates that GRB 230307A lasted much longer than what is typical of other bursts. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, "a reprieve" would be either a temporary relief from something or a delay of a punishment, neither of which would make sense as something that the duration of a gamma ray burst could provide to the burst itself. Choice C is incorrect. Although it would make sense to refer to gamma ray burst GRB 230307A itself as "an incident," or a thing that occurred, the missing word describes the duration of the burst, and it doesn&rsquo;t make much sense to describe a length of time as an incident. Further, the sentence emphasizes that the burst&rsquo;s duration was very unusual, not simply that the burst occurred.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dc234d74","external_id":"a1e8699f-954c-4f4e-b0ea-95f400636c11","vaultid":null,"uId":"8c72ad63-567b-4583-a669-a4cefbe9d5b5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The recently observed gamma ray burst GRB 230307A lasted for 200 seconds, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> for a burst generated by the merger of neutron stars. Bursts caused by neutron mergers typically last fewer than 2 seconds.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b448cb13-81d7-4cbf-89b8-1ac8a1378b82","content":"<p>a coincidence&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"c4a681e7-90a3-4346-9880-4b8810306f46","content":"<p>a reprieve</p>"},{"id":"f30f28bf-28cf-471d-908f-39c7adc555b6","content":"<p>an incident</p>"},{"id":"3cfe237b-f66d-4a0a-9f7d-c487b6b6853a","content":"<p>an oddity</p>"}],"keys":["3cfe237b-f66d-4a0a-9f7d-c487b6b6853a"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the duration of gamma ray burst GRB 230307A. In this context, \"an oddity\" is something that is odd or unusual. The text explains that the burst lasted for 200 seconds and that other bursts generated by neutron mergers have usually lasted fewer than 2 seconds. In other words, the duration of gamma ray burst GRB 230307A was unusual.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text focuses on a difference between the duration of gamma ray burst GRB 230307A and the typical duration of bursts caused by neutron mergers, not \"a coincidence,\" or a point of correspondence, between them; the text indicates that GRB 230307A lasted much longer than what is typical of other bursts. Choice B is incorrect. In this context, \"a reprieve\" would be either a temporary relief from something or a delay of a punishment, neither of which would make sense as something that the duration of a gamma ray burst could provide to the burst itself. Choice C is incorrect. Although it would make sense to refer to gamma ray burst GRB 230307A itself as \"an incident,\" or a thing that occurred, the missing word describes the duration of the burst, and it doesn&rsquo;t make much sense to describe a length of time as an incident. Further, the sentence emphasizes that the burst&rsquo;s duration was very unusual, not simply that the burst occurred.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669211,"updateDate":1743430669211}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669211, 1743430669211),
    ($SATQ$dcd9ad50$SATQ$, $SATQ$2e32460e-928a-4600-ba16-c68c23d2d0df$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from George Eliot&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1857</span> short story &ldquo;The Sad Fortunes of the <span data-ssml-sub-alias="Reverend">Rev.</span> Amos Barton.&rdquo; <span data-ssml-sub-alias="Mr">Mr.</span> Ely is a clergyman in the town of Milby.&nbsp;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By the laity of Milby and its neighbourhood [<span data-ssml-sub-alias="Mr">Mr.</span> Ely] was regarded as a man of quite remarkable powers and learning, who must make a considerable sensation in London pulpits and drawing-rooms on his occasional visit to the metropolis; and by his brother clergy he was regarded as a discreet and agreeable fellow. <span data-ssml-sub-alias="Mr">Mr.</span> Ely never got into a warm discussion; he suggested what might be thought, but rarely said what he thought himself; he never let either men or women see that he was laughing at them, and he never gave any one an opportunity of laughing at <em>him</em>.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ec9b78b8-1356-4788-865c-5bbce29ce05e","content":"<p>It shows that <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely had originally been held in high regard by his friends and then details the events that caused their regard for him to subside.</p>"},{"id":"1fd95921-2b64-4547-97a0-49126d7248f1","content":"<p>It implies that <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely&rsquo;s neighbors are more na&iuml;ve in their estimation of him than people in London are and then explains why his neighbors have been so easily misled.</p>"},{"id":"a1257568-d5bb-4896-a827-9d42833c611b","content":"<p>It stresses the discrepancy between <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely&rsquo;s public and private conduct and then alludes to his motivation for hiding his true personality.</p>"},{"id":"31e26ebe-5cee-41cf-8c58-bee46e3f4d3e","content":"<p>It presents the favorable opinion of <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely that other people hold and then describes the behaviors of <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely that enable him to maintain that favorable opinion.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["31e26ebe-5cee-41cf-8c58-bee46e3f4d3e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the overall structure of the text. The first part of the text presents the favorable opinions that others hold of Mr. Ely: the laity of Milby regard him as having "remarkable powers and learning" and assume that he also impresses people in London, while his fellow clergy see him as "a discreet and agreeable fellow." The text then goes on to describe Mr. Ely&rsquo;s behaviors that enable him to cultivate and preserve his positive reputation. The text conveys that he avoids confrontations, abstaining from "warm discussion[s]" (that is, heated or contentious ones); that he avoids offending anyone by "rarely [saying] what he thought himself" and by refraining from openly ridiculing anyone; and that he doesn&rsquo;t give anyone any reason to ridicule or belittle his own character ("he never gave any one an opportunity of laughing at <em>him</em>"). In other words, these are all behaviors that suggest that Mr. Ely avoids doing anything that might make people dislike him or think poorly of him. Thus, the text begins by presenting the favorable view that people have of Mr. Ely and then describes the behaviors that enable him to maintain that favorable view.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe any events that caused people&rsquo;s regard for Mr. Ely to diminish. Instead, the text explains that the people of Milby hold him in high regard and describes behaviors that suggest how he cultivates that regard. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Mr. Ely&rsquo;s neighbors are more na&iuml;ve than Londoners are or that they&rsquo;ve been misled. Although the text suggests that Mr. Ely is very guarded in his outward behavior and conceals from people when he may be internally mocking them, which might suggest that people know him less well than they think they do, there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that Londoners are less na&iuml;ve and therefore less easily misled than Mr. Ely&rsquo;s neighbors are. The text states that the people of Milby believe Mr. Ely to be "remarkable" and that they assume that people in London must find him so, too; it doesn&rsquo;t say anything about what Londoners actually think of him. Choice C is incorrect. While the text discusses Mr. Ely&rsquo;s public conduct at length, describing the ways in which he carefully cultivates an agreeable public persona, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how Mr. Ely behaves in private at all; it therefore wouldn&rsquo;t be accurate to say that the text begins by emphasizing a discrepancy between his public and private conduct. Furthermore, although the text concludes with examples illustrating that Mr. Ely typically doesn&rsquo;t share his true thoughts, indicating that he may be hiding aspects of his true personality when in public, and although the text as a whole conveys the idea that Mr. Ely benefits from this behavior, which might hint at his motives for doing so, the text doesn&rsquo;t conclude by alluding to those motives. Instead, the text merely concludes with a description of the behaviors he engages in to win his neighbors&rsquo; and colleagues&rsquo; approval.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dcd9ad50","external_id":"2e32460e-928a-4600-ba16-c68c23d2d0df","vaultid":null,"uId":"fc8b9029-ef6e-41af-b781-9a6e1d7d99a9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from George Eliot&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1857</span> short story &ldquo;The Sad Fortunes of the <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Reverend\">Rev.</span> Amos Barton.&rdquo; <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely is a clergyman in the town of Milby.&nbsp;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>By the laity of Milby and its neighbourhood [<span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely] was regarded as a man of quite remarkable powers and learning, who must make a considerable sensation in London pulpits and drawing-rooms on his occasional visit to the metropolis; and by his brother clergy he was regarded as a discreet and agreeable fellow. <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely never got into a warm discussion; he suggested what might be thought, but rarely said what he thought himself; he never let either men or women see that he was laughing at them, and he never gave any one an opportunity of laughing at <em>him</em>.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ec9b78b8-1356-4788-865c-5bbce29ce05e","content":"<p>It shows that <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely had originally been held in high regard by his friends and then details the events that caused their regard for him to subside.</p>"},{"id":"1fd95921-2b64-4547-97a0-49126d7248f1","content":"<p>It implies that <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely&rsquo;s neighbors are more na&iuml;ve in their estimation of him than people in London are and then explains why his neighbors have been so easily misled.</p>"},{"id":"a1257568-d5bb-4896-a827-9d42833c611b","content":"<p>It stresses the discrepancy between <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely&rsquo;s public and private conduct and then alludes to his motivation for hiding his true personality.</p>"},{"id":"31e26ebe-5cee-41cf-8c58-bee46e3f4d3e","content":"<p>It presents the favorable opinion of <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely that other people hold and then describes the behaviors of <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"Mr\">Mr.</span> Ely that enable him to maintain that favorable opinion.</p>"}],"keys":["31e26ebe-5cee-41cf-8c58-bee46e3f4d3e"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes the overall structure of the text. The first part of the text presents the favorable opinions that others hold of Mr. Ely: the laity of Milby regard him as having \"remarkable powers and learning\" and assume that he also impresses people in London, while his fellow clergy see him as \"a discreet and agreeable fellow.\" The text then goes on to describe Mr. Ely&rsquo;s behaviors that enable him to cultivate and preserve his positive reputation. The text conveys that he avoids confrontations, abstaining from \"warm discussion[s]\" (that is, heated or contentious ones); that he avoids offending anyone by \"rarely [saying] what he thought himself\" and by refraining from openly ridiculing anyone; and that he doesn&rsquo;t give anyone any reason to ridicule or belittle his own character (\"he never gave any one an opportunity of laughing at <em>him</em>\"). In other words, these are all behaviors that suggest that Mr. Ely avoids doing anything that might make people dislike him or think poorly of him. Thus, the text begins by presenting the favorable view that people have of Mr. Ely and then describes the behaviors that enable him to maintain that favorable view.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t describe any events that caused people&rsquo;s regard for Mr. Ely to diminish. Instead, the text explains that the people of Milby hold him in high regard and describes behaviors that suggest how he cultivates that regard. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Mr. Ely&rsquo;s neighbors are more na&iuml;ve than Londoners are or that they&rsquo;ve been misled. Although the text suggests that Mr. Ely is very guarded in his outward behavior and conceals from people when he may be internally mocking them, which might suggest that people know him less well than they think they do, there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that Londoners are less na&iuml;ve and therefore less easily misled than Mr. Ely&rsquo;s neighbors are. The text states that the people of Milby believe Mr. Ely to be \"remarkable\" and that they assume that people in London must find him so, too; it doesn&rsquo;t say anything about what Londoners actually think of him. Choice C is incorrect. While the text discusses Mr. Ely&rsquo;s public conduct at length, describing the ways in which he carefully cultivates an agreeable public persona, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how Mr. Ely behaves in private at all; it therefore wouldn&rsquo;t be accurate to say that the text begins by emphasizing a discrepancy between his public and private conduct. Furthermore, although the text concludes with examples illustrating that Mr. Ely typically doesn&rsquo;t share his true thoughts, indicating that he may be hiding aspects of his true personality when in public, and although the text as a whole conveys the idea that Mr. Ely benefits from this behavior, which might hint at his motives for doing so, the text doesn&rsquo;t conclude by alluding to those motives. Instead, the text merely concludes with a description of the behaviors he engages in to win his neighbors&rsquo; and colleagues&rsquo; approval.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$dd19befa$SATQ$, $SATQ$654af45e-67f9-45d0-a343-f3784da126af$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The familial structure of the black swan (<em>Cygnus atratus</em>) shows tremendous <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: breeding pairs with demonstrated success in rearing cygnets (young swans) have been observed to foster those of less successful pairs, and while rearing by female-male pairs is most common, male-male pairs sometimes rear cygnets too, achieving a significantly higher rate of survival to adulthood than female-male pairs.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b1ecf796-fa04-4ffb-a4cb-9a3315ebd12e","content":"<p>cohesion</p>"},{"id":"d556fe35-2e20-4e0c-b886-af82c570773b","content":"<p>elasticity</p>"},{"id":"f636466e-80a1-45b4-a092-44d76b471ce9","content":"<p>affection</p>"},{"id":"8aa08edf-c300-44fb-a8fe-c4a8d9dcd104","content":"<p>reciprocity</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d556fe35-2e20-4e0c-b886-af82c570773b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of black swan familial structures. In this context, &ldquo;elasticity&rdquo; means flexibility or adaptability. The text provides examples of flexible family arrangements among black swans: successful male-female breeding pairs are known to foster other pairs&rsquo; cygnets, and while they are less common, male-male pairs also successfully rear cygnets. This context therefore emphasizes the flexibility or elasticity of black swan familial structures, showing how this trait contributes to the success of the species.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although close family units may show &ldquo;cohesion,&rdquo; which refers to unity or harmony within a group, the text&rsquo;s focus is on the variety and adaptability of black swan family structures. The examples given&mdash;fostering other pairs&rsquo; cygnets and forming male-male as well as female-male pairs&mdash;highlight flexibility rather than cohesion, emphasizing family structures that vary from the model in which a breeding pair raises its own young. Choice C is incorrect. While &ldquo;affection,&rdquo; or fondness, might be present in black swan relationships, the text doesn&rsquo;t focus on emotional bonds but rather on the structural flexibility of black swan familial arrangements. The examples provided demonstrate adaptable breeding and rearing practices rather than affectionate behaviors. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;reciprocity&rdquo; would suggest mutual exchange or cooperation between parties. Although the text mentions the fostering of cygnets by different successful breeding pairs, it doesn&rsquo;t establish that this is based on reciprocal exchanges. The focus is instead on the flexibility of family structures and the effectiveness of these varied structures in rearing young.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dd19befa","external_id":"654af45e-67f9-45d0-a343-f3784da126af","vaultid":null,"uId":"d6fe7ca6-5195-45ce-a71e-e8956da2b78d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The familial structure of the black swan (<em>Cygnus atratus</em>) shows tremendous <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: breeding pairs with demonstrated success in rearing cygnets (young swans) have been observed to foster those of less successful pairs, and while rearing by female-male pairs is most common, male-male pairs sometimes rear cygnets too, achieving a significantly higher rate of survival to adulthood than female-male pairs.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b1ecf796-fa04-4ffb-a4cb-9a3315ebd12e","content":"<p>cohesion</p>"},{"id":"d556fe35-2e20-4e0c-b886-af82c570773b","content":"<p>elasticity</p>"},{"id":"f636466e-80a1-45b4-a092-44d76b471ce9","content":"<p>affection</p>"},{"id":"8aa08edf-c300-44fb-a8fe-c4a8d9dcd104","content":"<p>reciprocity</p>"}],"keys":["d556fe35-2e20-4e0c-b886-af82c570773b"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of black swan familial structures. In this context, &ldquo;elasticity&rdquo; means flexibility or adaptability. The text provides examples of flexible family arrangements among black swans: successful male-female breeding pairs are known to foster other pairs&rsquo; cygnets, and while they are less common, male-male pairs also successfully rear cygnets. This context therefore emphasizes the flexibility or elasticity of black swan familial structures, showing how this trait contributes to the success of the species.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although close family units may show &ldquo;cohesion,&rdquo; which refers to unity or harmony within a group, the text&rsquo;s focus is on the variety and adaptability of black swan family structures. The examples given&mdash;fostering other pairs&rsquo; cygnets and forming male-male as well as female-male pairs&mdash;highlight flexibility rather than cohesion, emphasizing family structures that vary from the model in which a breeding pair raises its own young. Choice C is incorrect. While &ldquo;affection,&rdquo; or fondness, might be present in black swan relationships, the text doesn&rsquo;t focus on emotional bonds but rather on the structural flexibility of black swan familial arrangements. The examples provided demonstrate adaptable breeding and rearing practices rather than affectionate behaviors. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;reciprocity&rdquo; would suggest mutual exchange or cooperation between parties. Although the text mentions the fostering of cygnets by different successful breeding pairs, it doesn&rsquo;t establish that this is based on reciprocal exchanges. The focus is instead on the flexibility of family structures and the effectiveness of these varied structures in rearing young.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$de2c2f57$SATQ$, $SATQ$3e54bdde-9e2e-4bbe-a879-b08aa6838f8f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$68ba4681-75ac-43e0-a63d-49d5da46f961$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Text 1</p>
<p>The fossil record suggests that mammoths went extinct around 11 thousand years (kyr) ago. In a 2021 study of environmental DNA (eDNA)&mdash;genetic material shed into the environment by organisms&mdash;in the Arctic, Yucheng Wang and colleagues found mammoth eDNA in sedimentary layers formed millennia later, around 4 kyr ago. To account for this discrepancy, Joshua H. Miller and Carl Simpson proposed that arctic temperatures could preserve a mammoth carcass on the surface, allowing it to leach DNA into the environment, for several thousand years.</p>
<p>Text 2</p>
<p>Wang and colleagues concede that eDNA contains DNA from both living organisms and carcasses, but for DNA to leach from remains over several millennia requires that the remains be perpetually on the surface. Scavengers and weathering in the Arctic, however, are likely to break down surface remains well before a thousand years have passed.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes how Text 1 and Text 2 relate to each other?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6ba4558b-2c0c-4975-a815-608221708c40","content":"<p>Text 1 discusses two approaches to studying mammoth extinction without advocating for either, whereas Text 2 advocates for one approach over the other.</p>"},{"id":"6d0033df-8194-47cc-b3e8-bd3a5a1e7563","content":"<p>Text 1 presents findings by Wang and colleagues and gives another research team&rsquo;s attempt to explain those findings, whereas Text 2 provides additional detail that calls that explanation into question.</p>"},{"id":"e33235f1-5a83-4092-83a8-cdd3cbad8db3","content":"<p>Text 1 describes Wang and colleagues&rsquo; study and a critique of their methodology, whereas Text 2 offers additional details showing that methodology to be sound.</p>"},{"id":"66e8089d-8f34-46dd-b39d-1ff569995b4d","content":"<p>Text 1 argues that new research has undermined the standard view of when mammoths went extinct, whereas Text 2 suggests a way to reconcile the standard view with that new research.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6d0033df-8194-47cc-b3e8-bd3a5a1e7563"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Text 1 introduces Wang and colleagues&rsquo; study and its surprising results, and then mentions Miller and Simpson&rsquo;s hypothesis as a possible way to explain them. Text 2, however, challenges Miller and Simpson&rsquo;s hypothesis by pointing out the difficulties of preserving mammoth carcasses on the surface for thousands of years: &ldquo;scavengers and weathering&rdquo; are the additional details that complicate the Miller/Simpson hypothesis. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither text compares two different approaches for studying mammoth extinction. Text 1 describes one study and one hypothesis pertaining to it. Text 2 critiques that hypothesis. Choice C is incorrect. Text 1 does not describe a critique of Wang and colleagues&rsquo; methodology, but rather an interpretation of their results by Miller and Simpson. Text 2 does not offer additional details showing that methodology to be sound, but rather casts doubt on the Miller/Simpson explanation. Choice D is incorrect. Both components mentioned here (the new &ldquo;undermining&rdquo; research and the theory for reconciling this discovery) are contained in Text 1. Text 2 then shows how the attempt to reconcile the standard view and new research is flawed, and still fails to explain the discrepancy. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-H009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4e3d57a0-dd5a-4c6a-92a5-c441938b9626$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"de2c2f57","external_id":"3e54bdde-9e2e-4bbe-a879-b08aa6838f8f","vaultid":"68ba4681-75ac-43e0-a63d-49d5da46f961","uId":"d1aff7db-1c5a-4d72-aab3-8d4c3b3a2556","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Text 1</p>\n<p>The fossil record suggests that mammoths went extinct around 11 thousand years (kyr) ago. In a 2021 study of environmental DNA (eDNA)&mdash;genetic material shed into the environment by organisms&mdash;in the Arctic, Yucheng Wang and colleagues found mammoth eDNA in sedimentary layers formed millennia later, around 4 kyr ago. To account for this discrepancy, Joshua H. Miller and Carl Simpson proposed that arctic temperatures could preserve a mammoth carcass on the surface, allowing it to leach DNA into the environment, for several thousand years.</p>\n<p>Text 2</p>\n<p>Wang and colleagues concede that eDNA contains DNA from both living organisms and carcasses, but for DNA to leach from remains over several millennia requires that the remains be perpetually on the surface. Scavengers and weathering in the Arctic, however, are likely to break down surface remains well before a thousand years have passed.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes how Text 1 and Text 2 relate to each other?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6ba4558b-2c0c-4975-a815-608221708c40","content":"<p>Text 1 discusses two approaches to studying mammoth extinction without advocating for either, whereas Text 2 advocates for one approach over the other.</p>"},{"id":"6d0033df-8194-47cc-b3e8-bd3a5a1e7563","content":"<p>Text 1 presents findings by Wang and colleagues and gives another research team&rsquo;s attempt to explain those findings, whereas Text 2 provides additional detail that calls that explanation into question.</p>"},{"id":"e33235f1-5a83-4092-83a8-cdd3cbad8db3","content":"<p>Text 1 describes Wang and colleagues&rsquo; study and a critique of their methodology, whereas Text 2 offers additional details showing that methodology to be sound.</p>"},{"id":"66e8089d-8f34-46dd-b39d-1ff569995b4d","content":"<p>Text 1 argues that new research has undermined the standard view of when mammoths went extinct, whereas Text 2 suggests a way to reconcile the standard view with that new research.</p>"}],"keys":["6d0033df-8194-47cc-b3e8-bd3a5a1e7563"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Text 1 introduces Wang and colleagues&rsquo; study and its surprising results, and then mentions Miller and Simpson&rsquo;s hypothesis as a possible way to explain them. Text 2, however, challenges Miller and Simpson&rsquo;s hypothesis by pointing out the difficulties of preserving mammoth carcasses on the surface for thousands of years: &ldquo;scavengers and weathering&rdquo; are the additional details that complicate the Miller/Simpson hypothesis. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Neither text compares two different approaches for studying mammoth extinction. Text 1 describes one study and one hypothesis pertaining to it. Text 2 critiques that hypothesis. Choice C is incorrect. Text 1 does not describe a critique of Wang and colleagues&rsquo; methodology, but rather an interpretation of their results by Miller and Simpson. Text 2 does not offer additional details showing that methodology to be sound, but rather casts doubt on the Miller/Simpson explanation. Choice D is incorrect. Both components mentioned here (the new &ldquo;undermining&rdquo; research and the theory for reconciling this discovery) are contained in Text 1. Text 2 then shows how the attempt to reconcile the standard view and new research is flawed, and still fails to explain the discrepancy. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-H009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4e3d57a0-dd5a-4c6a-92a5-c441938b9626","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959365,"updateDate":1691007959365}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959365, 1691007959365),
    ($SATQ$df45f0eb$SATQ$, $SATQ$286bac89-75e1-4286-a693-0b58ff7fb444$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">Several studies have found negligible electoral consequences for governments that impose fiscal austerity measures, yet some European governments recently suffered electorally due to their austerity programs.</span> Evelyne Huebscher and colleagues attribute this incongruity to governments&rsquo; tendency&mdash;not followed in the recent European cases&mdash;to implement austerity programs strategically to avoid electoral costs (e.g., setting spending cuts to take effect only after the next election), which has obscured the inherent political risks of austerity measures in the election data scholars have examined.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d212dcab-f626-4095-96c8-5425f61a8670","content":"<p>It explains a discrepancy between what has been observed in study settings and what has been observed in real-world settings that the text goes on to assert is attributable to the studies not using real-world data.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3e2b883e-7558-4e7a-b595-bbb89c573572","content":"<p>It identifies a conflict between research findings and recent events that the text goes on to suggest is a consequence of a complicating factor in the data used to generate those findings.</p>"},{"id":"8ed0daa2-41d1-44b1-b7c8-ea9bb1a5c5a3","content":"<p>It presents a long-standing divergence in research findings that the text goes on to say is due to different groups of researchers using data that derive from different electoral circumstances.</p>"},{"id":"a1ecf938-6315-488a-9afe-6de4250cc32f","content":"<p>It describes a recent exception to a general pattern in research findings that the text goes on to explain is a result of researchers underestimating the significance of inconsistencies in the data they&rsquo;ve analyzed.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3e2b883e-7558-4e7a-b595-bbb89c573572"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The underlined sentence explains that contrary to what several studies would suggest, recent European governments suffered electorally after the launch of fiscal austerity programs. The text goes on to indicate that the researchers generated their findings from data that didn&rsquo;t reveal the true political risk of austerity measures because the data were based on cases in which governments had set austerity programs to take effect after the next election, a practice the European governments that recently suffered electorally didn&rsquo;t adhere to, thus introducing a complicating factor resulting in a conflict between the research findings and recent events.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the discrepancy described in the text is between observations made in study settings and observations made in real-world settings. Rather, the underlined sentence indicates that the outcome of recent events is contrary to what would be expected based on the findings of several studies. Additionally, there is nothing in the text to suggest that the studies mentioned did not use real-world data; instead, the text indicates that the data used was generated under potentially different circumstances than the recent events. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t present a long-standing divergence in research findings but rather a discrepancy between past research findings and recent events that the text goes on to attribute to researchers&rsquo; use of data that didn&rsquo;t reveal the true political risk of austerity measures. Choice D is incorrect because while the underlined sentence notes that there have been some recent exceptions to a general pattern observed in several research studies, it does not go on to attribute this exception to the researchers underestimating inconsistencies in the data. Rather, the text goes on to attribute this to a circumstance (fiscal austerity measures being implemented before an election rather than after) which adds a complicating factor into the data not accounted for in past studies.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"df45f0eb","external_id":"286bac89-75e1-4286-a693-0b58ff7fb444","vaultid":null,"uId":"a514995b-6afc-4b83-a9be-20a8fcc15cc0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">Several studies have found negligible electoral consequences for governments that impose fiscal austerity measures, yet some European governments recently suffered electorally due to their austerity programs.</span> Evelyne Huebscher and colleagues attribute this incongruity to governments&rsquo; tendency&mdash;not followed in the recent European cases&mdash;to implement austerity programs strategically to avoid electoral costs (e.g., setting spending cuts to take effect only after the next election), which has obscured the inherent political risks of austerity measures in the election data scholars have examined.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d212dcab-f626-4095-96c8-5425f61a8670","content":"<p>It explains a discrepancy between what has been observed in study settings and what has been observed in real-world settings that the text goes on to assert is attributable to the studies not using real-world data.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3e2b883e-7558-4e7a-b595-bbb89c573572","content":"<p>It identifies a conflict between research findings and recent events that the text goes on to suggest is a consequence of a complicating factor in the data used to generate those findings.</p>"},{"id":"8ed0daa2-41d1-44b1-b7c8-ea9bb1a5c5a3","content":"<p>It presents a long-standing divergence in research findings that the text goes on to say is due to different groups of researchers using data that derive from different electoral circumstances.</p>"},{"id":"a1ecf938-6315-488a-9afe-6de4250cc32f","content":"<p>It describes a recent exception to a general pattern in research findings that the text goes on to explain is a result of researchers underestimating the significance of inconsistencies in the data they&rsquo;ve analyzed.</p>"}],"keys":["3e2b883e-7558-4e7a-b595-bbb89c573572"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The underlined sentence explains that contrary to what several studies would suggest, recent European governments suffered electorally after the launch of fiscal austerity programs. The text goes on to indicate that the researchers generated their findings from data that didn&rsquo;t reveal the true political risk of austerity measures because the data were based on cases in which governments had set austerity programs to take effect after the next election, a practice the European governments that recently suffered electorally didn&rsquo;t adhere to, thus introducing a complicating factor resulting in a conflict between the research findings and recent events.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the discrepancy described in the text is between observations made in study settings and observations made in real-world settings. Rather, the underlined sentence indicates that the outcome of recent events is contrary to what would be expected based on the findings of several studies. Additionally, there is nothing in the text to suggest that the studies mentioned did not use real-world data; instead, the text indicates that the data used was generated under potentially different circumstances than the recent events. Choice C is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t present a long-standing divergence in research findings but rather a discrepancy between past research findings and recent events that the text goes on to attribute to researchers&rsquo; use of data that didn&rsquo;t reveal the true political risk of austerity measures. Choice D is incorrect because while the underlined sentence notes that there have been some recent exceptions to a general pattern observed in several research studies, it does not go on to attribute this exception to the researchers underestimating inconsistencies in the data. Rather, the text goes on to attribute this to a circumstance (fiscal austerity measures being implemented before an election rather than after) which adds a complicating factor into the data not accounted for in past studies.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323040,"updateDate":1730147323040}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323040, 1730147323040),
    ($SATQ$df46a2ee$SATQ$, $SATQ$76bf8cd6-f573-4f30-a750-5030c3070924$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a6ec87da-7386-47ed-b022-26f3b99a0d11$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Joseph Conrad&rsquo;s 1907 novel <em>The Secret Agent: A Simple Tale. </em>Mr. Verloc is navigating the London streets on his way to a meeting.</p>
<p>Before reaching Knightsbridge, Mr. Verloc took a turn to the left out of the busy main thoroughfare, uproarious with the traffic of swaying omnibuses and trotting vans, in the almost silent, swift flow of hansoms [horse-drawn carriages]. Under his hat, worn with a slight backward tilt, his hair had been carefully brushed into respectful sleekness; for his business was with an Embassy. And Mr. Verloc, steady like a rock&mdash;<u>a soft kind of rock</u>&mdash;marched now along a street which could with every propriety be described as private.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined phrase in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"84f90017-757e-4487-afec-652677866834","content":"<p>It qualifies an earlier description of Mr. Verloc.</p>"},{"id":"e03afb9b-5042-4e79-b101-a23c6f4fa23b","content":"<p>It emphasizes an internal struggle Mr. Verloc experiences.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"d099c1f8-d6f3-479b-87c6-61f58b799200","content":"<p>It contrasts Mr. Verloc with his surroundings.</p>"},{"id":"383eb7a6-d25a-415f-9ca9-4d502d3ee9d3","content":"<p>It reveals a private opinion Mr. Verloc holds.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["84f90017-757e-4487-afec-652677866834"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The underline phrase qualifies (meaning adds limits or conditions to) the description of Mr. Verloc as &ldquo;steady like a rock,&rdquo; adding that he is a &ldquo;soft&rdquo; rock. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. In fact, the passage never mentions Mr. Verloc experiencing any internal struggles. Choice C is incorrect. The underlined phrase doesn&rsquo;t contrast Mr. Verloc with his surroundings, but is instead modifying the description of him as a rock. Choice D is incorrect. The underlined phrase doesn&rsquo;t reveal a private opinion Mr. Verloc holds: instead, it further describes his character for the reader. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-H005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ff0b7407-6043-4014-8f7c-acaeffea0c6d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"df46a2ee","external_id":"76bf8cd6-f573-4f30-a750-5030c3070924","vaultid":"a6ec87da-7386-47ed-b022-26f3b99a0d11","uId":"3273611e-e72f-439b-90f4-26966e30cbe4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Joseph Conrad&rsquo;s 1907 novel <em>The Secret Agent: A Simple Tale. </em>Mr. Verloc is navigating the London streets on his way to a meeting.</p>\n<p>Before reaching Knightsbridge, Mr. Verloc took a turn to the left out of the busy main thoroughfare, uproarious with the traffic of swaying omnibuses and trotting vans, in the almost silent, swift flow of hansoms [horse-drawn carriages]. Under his hat, worn with a slight backward tilt, his hair had been carefully brushed into respectful sleekness; for his business was with an Embassy. And Mr. Verloc, steady like a rock&mdash;<u>a soft kind of rock</u>&mdash;marched now along a street which could with every propriety be described as private.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined phrase in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"84f90017-757e-4487-afec-652677866834","content":"<p>It qualifies an earlier description of Mr. Verloc.</p>"},{"id":"e03afb9b-5042-4e79-b101-a23c6f4fa23b","content":"<p>It emphasizes an internal struggle Mr. Verloc experiences.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"d099c1f8-d6f3-479b-87c6-61f58b799200","content":"<p>It contrasts Mr. Verloc with his surroundings.</p>"},{"id":"383eb7a6-d25a-415f-9ca9-4d502d3ee9d3","content":"<p>It reveals a private opinion Mr. Verloc holds.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["84f90017-757e-4487-afec-652677866834"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The underline phrase qualifies (meaning adds limits or conditions to) the description of Mr. Verloc as &ldquo;steady like a rock,&rdquo; adding that he is a &ldquo;soft&rdquo; rock. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. In fact, the passage never mentions Mr. Verloc experiencing any internal struggles. Choice C is incorrect. The underlined phrase doesn&rsquo;t contrast Mr. Verloc with his surroundings, but is instead modifying the description of him as a rock. Choice D is incorrect. The underlined phrase doesn&rsquo;t reveal a private opinion Mr. Verloc holds: instead, it further describes his character for the reader. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-H005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ff0b7407-6043-4014-8f7c-acaeffea0c6d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959375,"updateDate":1691007959375}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959375, 1691007959375),
    ($SATQ$e0656211$SATQ$, $SATQ$270aa465-e0d8-43f8-b2f5-6a42058a1721$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d764c3f3-b47b-467b-bbba-23d13fc64c0f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In <em>Nature Poem</em> (2017), Kumeyaay poet Tommy Pico portrays his <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the natural world by honoring the centrality of nature within his tribe&rsquo;s traditional beliefs while simultaneously expressing his distaste for being in wilderness settings himself.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ea12e474-6ba5-43a6-a30e-8be0c7d77c4b","content":"<p>responsiveness to</p>"},{"id":"2c82e679-9574-4523-afdd-1aee27bc94d6","content":"<p>ambivalence toward</p>"},{"id":"551f7358-2c45-4f34-955d-2a39be37611e","content":"<p>renunciation of</p>"},{"id":"fe2486c6-5085-4d18-8d2c-e844b9fe9e89","content":"<p>mastery over</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2c82e679-9574-4523-afdd-1aee27bc94d6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s description of how Pico feels about the natural world. In this context, to say that Pico portrays his &ldquo;ambivalence toward&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays his mixed feelings about nature. The text explains that Pico &ldquo;honors the centrality of nature&rdquo; and also makes it clear that he doesn&rsquo;t enjoy being in nature. This context suggests that Pico feels ambivalence toward nature. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because saying that Pico portrays his &ldquo;responsiveness to&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays himself as quick to react to nature, which isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. Instead, the text focuses on Pico&rsquo;s mixed feelings toward nature, describing him as both honoring nature&rsquo;s role in his tribe&rsquo;s beliefs and expressing his personal dislike for being in nature.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because saying that Pico portrays his &ldquo;renunciation of&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays himself as rejecting nature, which isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. The text conveys that Pico demonstrates both positive and negative responses toward nature, not that he&rsquo;s giving it up completely.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because saying that Pico portrays his &ldquo;mastery over&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays himself as having control over nature, which isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. The text focuses on Pico&rsquo;s mixed feelings about nature; nothing in the text suggests that Pico feels mastery over nature. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-978-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$99f6e534-64dd-41ae-a9ea-0ada618052a7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e0656211","external_id":"270aa465-e0d8-43f8-b2f5-6a42058a1721","vaultid":"d764c3f3-b47b-467b-bbba-23d13fc64c0f","uId":"3e21fe0d-ce3e-4e1c-a674-4397ef8315bd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In <em>Nature Poem</em> (2017), Kumeyaay poet Tommy Pico portrays his <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the natural world by honoring the centrality of nature within his tribe&rsquo;s traditional beliefs while simultaneously expressing his distaste for being in wilderness settings himself.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ea12e474-6ba5-43a6-a30e-8be0c7d77c4b","content":"<p>responsiveness to</p>"},{"id":"2c82e679-9574-4523-afdd-1aee27bc94d6","content":"<p>ambivalence toward</p>"},{"id":"551f7358-2c45-4f34-955d-2a39be37611e","content":"<p>renunciation of</p>"},{"id":"fe2486c6-5085-4d18-8d2c-e844b9fe9e89","content":"<p>mastery over</p>"}],"keys":["2c82e679-9574-4523-afdd-1aee27bc94d6"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s description of how Pico feels about the natural world. In this context, to say that Pico portrays his &ldquo;ambivalence toward&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays his mixed feelings about nature. The text explains that Pico &ldquo;honors the centrality of nature&rdquo; and also makes it clear that he doesn&rsquo;t enjoy being in nature. This context suggests that Pico feels ambivalence toward nature. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because saying that Pico portrays his &ldquo;responsiveness to&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays himself as quick to react to nature, which isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. Instead, the text focuses on Pico&rsquo;s mixed feelings toward nature, describing him as both honoring nature&rsquo;s role in his tribe&rsquo;s beliefs and expressing his personal dislike for being in nature.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because saying that Pico portrays his &ldquo;renunciation of&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays himself as rejecting nature, which isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. The text conveys that Pico demonstrates both positive and negative responses toward nature, not that he&rsquo;s giving it up completely.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because saying that Pico portrays his &ldquo;mastery over&rdquo; nature would mean that he portrays himself as having control over nature, which isn&rsquo;t supported by the text. The text focuses on Pico&rsquo;s mixed feelings about nature; nothing in the text suggests that Pico feels mastery over nature. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-978-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"99f6e534-64dd-41ae-a9ea-0ada618052a7","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$e13171c4$SATQ$, $SATQ$166b4da7-a028-4e27-afcc-a6087b2bd8af$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0b6fd534-3878-4a11-a92b-ed755e975ab8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Historians Tiya Miles and Roy E. Finkenbine have both documented the assistance Indigenous peoples gave to Black freedom seekers leaving the South before the US Civil War. Much of the historical evidence of this help comes from Indigenous oral traditions and from autobiographies written by the freedom seekers.&nbsp;<u>One such narrative is Jermain Loguen&rsquo;s autobiography, which tells about how Neshnab&eacute; (Potawatomi) villagers offered him food, lodging, and directions during his 1835 journey from Tennessee to Canada.</u></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"956ba943-fae6-4928-bc07-22bb39327812","content":"<p>It provides an example of an autobiography that describes help given by an Indigenous people to a Black freedom seeker.</p>"},{"id":"88effe3c-95cb-464f-a360-b2edffd4dd79","content":"<p>It shows why Loguen decided to write in great detail about his experiences traveling from Tennessee to Canada in his autobiography.</p>"},{"id":"183ef5a8-4827-4874-a144-ba850e08f611","content":"<p>It argues that autobiographies are particularly important sources of information about geography in the United States before the Civil War.</p>"},{"id":"828c63cf-22ab-4079-9d40-258a40e5e6ee","content":"<p>It suggests that most historians believe that Neshnab&eacute; villagers were more successful in assisting freedom seekers than other people were.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["956ba943-fae6-4928-bc07-22bb39327812"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The previous sentence broadly mentions "autobiographies written by the freedom seekers." This sentence identifies Loguen&rsquo;s autobiography as a specific example.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence never explains why Loguen chose to write about his experiences. Choice C is incorrect. The previous sentence identifies autobiographies as useful sources of historical information about a specific topic, but not for "information about geography." The underlined sentence provides details of one autobiography as an example of a source of information about that specific topic (interactions between Indigenous people and Black freedom seekers). Choice D is incorrect. The text never discusses other specific people who helped freedom seekers, and therefore can&rsquo;t make a comparison between the Neshnab&eacute; and anyone else.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-E009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$85f06efe-b198-49af-bcdd-0c9528fc9382$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e13171c4","external_id":"166b4da7-a028-4e27-afcc-a6087b2bd8af","vaultid":"0b6fd534-3878-4a11-a92b-ed755e975ab8","uId":"2d6912cd-b169-4b29-8eda-a71d3b4be08d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Historians Tiya Miles and Roy E. Finkenbine have both documented the assistance Indigenous peoples gave to Black freedom seekers leaving the South before the US Civil War. Much of the historical evidence of this help comes from Indigenous oral traditions and from autobiographies written by the freedom seekers.&nbsp;<u>One such narrative is Jermain Loguen&rsquo;s autobiography, which tells about how Neshnab&eacute; (Potawatomi) villagers offered him food, lodging, and directions during his 1835 journey from Tennessee to Canada.</u></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"956ba943-fae6-4928-bc07-22bb39327812","content":"<p>It provides an example of an autobiography that describes help given by an Indigenous people to a Black freedom seeker.</p>"},{"id":"88effe3c-95cb-464f-a360-b2edffd4dd79","content":"<p>It shows why Loguen decided to write in great detail about his experiences traveling from Tennessee to Canada in his autobiography.</p>"},{"id":"183ef5a8-4827-4874-a144-ba850e08f611","content":"<p>It argues that autobiographies are particularly important sources of information about geography in the United States before the Civil War.</p>"},{"id":"828c63cf-22ab-4079-9d40-258a40e5e6ee","content":"<p>It suggests that most historians believe that Neshnab&eacute; villagers were more successful in assisting freedom seekers than other people were.</p>"}],"keys":["956ba943-fae6-4928-bc07-22bb39327812"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The previous sentence broadly mentions \"autobiographies written by the freedom seekers.\" This sentence identifies Loguen&rsquo;s autobiography as a specific example.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence never explains why Loguen chose to write about his experiences. Choice C is incorrect. The previous sentence identifies autobiographies as useful sources of historical information about a specific topic, but not for \"information about geography.\" The underlined sentence provides details of one autobiography as an example of a source of information about that specific topic (interactions between Indigenous people and Black freedom seekers). Choice D is incorrect. The text never discusses other specific people who helped freedom seekers, and therefore can&rsquo;t make a comparison between the Neshnab&eacute; and anyone else.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-E009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"85f06efe-b198-49af-bcdd-0c9528fc9382","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959374,"updateDate":1691007959374}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959374, 1691007959374),
    ($SATQ$e1befb41$SATQ$, $SATQ$4cdb7643-8ee1-4dc5-b7f3-5d6e6ce61444$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f22a069e-1e86-43a7-a1b3-da74154dcccc$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>In a study of the benefits of having free time, Marissa Sharif found that the reported sense of life satisfaction tended to plateau when participants had two hours of free time per day and actually began to fall when they had five hours of free time per day. After further research, Sharif concluded that this dip in life satisfaction mainly occurred when individuals spent all their free time unproductively, such as by watching TV or playing games.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level="2" role="heading"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>Psychologist James Maddux cautions against suggesting an ideal amount of free time. The human desire for both free time and productivity is universal, but Maddux asserts that individuals have unique needs for life satisfaction. Furthermore, he points out that there is no objective definition for what constitutes productivity; reading a book might be considered a productive activity by some, but idleness by others.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would Maddux (Text 2) most likely respond to the conclusion Sharif (Text 1) reached after her further research?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c8a40848-6b94-4538-9011-536faeb13b1e","content":"<p>By acknowledging that free time is more likely to enhance life satisfaction when it is spent productively than when it is spent unproductively</p>"},{"id":"c470d641-3ee6-42c1-ba2a-c82dbb166a6f","content":"<p>By challenging the reasoning in Text 1, as it has not been proved that productivity commonly contributes to individuals&rsquo; life satisfaction</p>"},{"id":"9351899c-b712-421f-8cbe-7686dc7f3ac7","content":"<p>By warning against making an overly broad assumption, as there is no clear consensus in distinguishing between productive and unproductive activities</p>"},{"id":"dbe23c03-3d3c-406a-ba82-4eeb17a05b73","content":"<p>By claiming that the specific activities named in Text 1 are actually examples of productive activities rather than unproductive ones</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9351899c-b712-421f-8cbe-7686dc7f3ac7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it characterizes how Maddux would most likely respond to the conclusion Sharif reached after her research. Text 1 describes Sharif&rsquo;s study of the benefits of free time, saying that the reported sense of satisfaction plateaued at two hours per day and began to decline at five hours per day. Further research led Sharif to conclude that time spent doing tasks she defines as unproductive, such as watching TV or playing games, correlated with a drop in life satisfaction. &nbsp;However, in Text 2 Maddux says that there is no objective definition of what constitutes productive behavior, giving the example that reading a book might be considered productive by some but unproductive by others. It can be inferred that Maddux would also assert that whether watching TV or playing games is productive or unproductive is a matter of subjective judgment. Thus, Maddux would most likely caution against making an overly broad assumption, as there is no clear consensus in distinguishing between productive and unproductive activities. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Maddux asserts that individuals have unique needs for life satisfaction: some may want to spend that time productively, others unproductively, and what counts as productive is subjective. Therefore, Maddux would likely not consider it universally true that free time is more likely to enhance life satisfaction when it is spent productively. Choice B is incorrect because the study described in Text 1 concerns whether free time contributes to life satisfaction, not whether productivity contributes to life satisfaction. The dip in life satisfaction that Sharif claims to observe in Text 1 happens only after five hours, and mainly if the time is spent unproductively&mdash;that is, two hours of free time spent productively might increase life satisfaction just as much as two hours spent unproductively. Choice D is incorrect because Maddux holds the opinion that whether an activity is productive or unproductive is subjective and depends on the individual; therefore, he would most likely claim that watching TV or playing games might be productive for some and unproductive for others. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-010-CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$faca7399-ae96-422b-a17d-a427f707465f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e1befb41","external_id":"4cdb7643-8ee1-4dc5-b7f3-5d6e6ce61444","vaultid":"f22a069e-1e86-43a7-a1b3-da74154dcccc","uId":"551a021b-0569-4db4-9a33-d777667696f9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>In a study of the benefits of having free time, Marissa Sharif found that the reported sense of life satisfaction tended to plateau when participants had two hours of free time per day and actually began to fall when they had five hours of free time per day. After further research, Sharif concluded that this dip in life satisfaction mainly occurred when individuals spent all their free time unproductively, such as by watching TV or playing games.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p><p><span aria-level=\"2\" role=\"heading\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>Psychologist James Maddux cautions against suggesting an ideal amount of free time. The human desire for both free time and productivity is universal, but Maddux asserts that individuals have unique needs for life satisfaction. Furthermore, he points out that there is no objective definition for what constitutes productivity; reading a book might be considered a productive activity by some, but idleness by others.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would Maddux (Text 2) most likely respond to the conclusion Sharif (Text 1) reached after her further research?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c8a40848-6b94-4538-9011-536faeb13b1e","content":"<p>By acknowledging that free time is more likely to enhance life satisfaction when it is spent productively than when it is spent unproductively</p>"},{"id":"c470d641-3ee6-42c1-ba2a-c82dbb166a6f","content":"<p>By challenging the reasoning in Text 1, as it has not been proved that productivity commonly contributes to individuals&rsquo; life satisfaction</p>"},{"id":"9351899c-b712-421f-8cbe-7686dc7f3ac7","content":"<p>By warning against making an overly broad assumption, as there is no clear consensus in distinguishing between productive and unproductive activities</p>"},{"id":"dbe23c03-3d3c-406a-ba82-4eeb17a05b73","content":"<p>By claiming that the specific activities named in Text 1 are actually examples of productive activities rather than unproductive ones</p>"}],"keys":["9351899c-b712-421f-8cbe-7686dc7f3ac7"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it characterizes how Maddux would most likely respond to the conclusion Sharif reached after her research. Text 1 describes Sharif&rsquo;s study of the benefits of free time, saying that the reported sense of satisfaction plateaued at two hours per day and began to decline at five hours per day. Further research led Sharif to conclude that time spent doing tasks she defines as unproductive, such as watching TV or playing games, correlated with a drop in life satisfaction. &nbsp;However, in Text 2 Maddux says that there is no objective definition of what constitutes productive behavior, giving the example that reading a book might be considered productive by some but unproductive by others. It can be inferred that Maddux would also assert that whether watching TV or playing games is productive or unproductive is a matter of subjective judgment. Thus, Maddux would most likely caution against making an overly broad assumption, as there is no clear consensus in distinguishing between productive and unproductive activities. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Maddux asserts that individuals have unique needs for life satisfaction: some may want to spend that time productively, others unproductively, and what counts as productive is subjective. Therefore, Maddux would likely not consider it universally true that free time is more likely to enhance life satisfaction when it is spent productively. Choice B is incorrect because the study described in Text 1 concerns whether free time contributes to life satisfaction, not whether productivity contributes to life satisfaction. The dip in life satisfaction that Sharif claims to observe in Text 1 happens only after five hours, and mainly if the time is spent unproductively&mdash;that is, two hours of free time spent productively might increase life satisfaction just as much as two hours spent unproductively. Choice D is incorrect because Maddux holds the opinion that whether an activity is productive or unproductive is subjective and depends on the individual; therefore, he would most likely claim that watching TV or playing games might be productive for some and unproductive for others. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-010-CTC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"faca7399-ae96-422b-a17d-a427f707465f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959314,"updateDate":1691007959314}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959314, 1691007959314),
    ($SATQ$e1d5d5df$SATQ$, $SATQ$74ad1b5e-86aa-4896-b0cc-5c8fd88485c2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$fa527611-05c5-47cc-bf78-b5bcda0b0270$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to botanists, a viburnum plant experiencing insect damage may develop erineum&mdash;a discolored, felty growth&mdash;on its leaf blades. A <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> viburnum plant, on the other hand, will have leaves with smooth surfaces and uniformly green coloration.  </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"86d8fe39-2ba5-4bfe-9ff9-663e0c04a468","content":"<p>struggling</p>"},{"id":"7fead038-56c9-47f6-ab81-fb922b10b7b6","content":"<p>beneficial</p>"},{"id":"f6705f54-9702-4360-864b-5b94a514c32a","content":"<p>simple</p>"},{"id":"348581ba-1fcf-4513-8ec0-14b74705c0f3","content":"<p>healthy</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["348581ba-1fcf-4513-8ec0-14b74705c0f3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of damage to viburnum plants. In this context, &ldquo;healthy&rdquo; would mean not distressed or diseased. The text states that insect damage may cause viburnum plants to be discolored and have abnormal growths. In the next sentence, the phrase &ldquo;on the other hand&rdquo; indicates a contrast with the description of plants suffering from damage. Thus, the context contrasts the appearance of healthy, undamaged plants with the appearance of damaged plants. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;struggling&rdquo; would mean working against difficulties. The text first describes viburnum plants experiencing damage by insects, and the phrase &ldquo;on the other hand&rdquo; then establishes a contrast with that description. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to contrast struggling viburnum plants with those being damaged by insects, because in both cases the plants would be experiencing difficulties. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;beneficial&rdquo; would mean producing good or helpful effects. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how viburnum plants affect other things or suggest that the plants are helpful in some way; rather, it focuses on how viburnum plants are affected by certain conditions. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;simple&rdquo; would mean plain or uncomplicated. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss whether certain viburnum plants are complicated or uncomplicated; rather, it focuses on how viburnum plants are affected by certain conditions. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-962-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9f524549-a6cd-490e-b543-daa2c125e718$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e1d5d5df","external_id":"74ad1b5e-86aa-4896-b0cc-5c8fd88485c2","vaultid":"fa527611-05c5-47cc-bf78-b5bcda0b0270","uId":"13c9d491-493d-49a6-a485-b139326aec4e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to botanists, a viburnum plant experiencing insect damage may develop erineum&mdash;a discolored, felty growth&mdash;on its leaf blades. A <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> viburnum plant, on the other hand, will have leaves with smooth surfaces and uniformly green coloration.  </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"86d8fe39-2ba5-4bfe-9ff9-663e0c04a468","content":"<p>struggling</p>"},{"id":"7fead038-56c9-47f6-ab81-fb922b10b7b6","content":"<p>beneficial</p>"},{"id":"f6705f54-9702-4360-864b-5b94a514c32a","content":"<p>simple</p>"},{"id":"348581ba-1fcf-4513-8ec0-14b74705c0f3","content":"<p>healthy</p>"}],"keys":["348581ba-1fcf-4513-8ec0-14b74705c0f3"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of damage to viburnum plants. In this context, &ldquo;healthy&rdquo; would mean not distressed or diseased. The text states that insect damage may cause viburnum plants to be discolored and have abnormal growths. In the next sentence, the phrase &ldquo;on the other hand&rdquo; indicates a contrast with the description of plants suffering from damage. Thus, the context contrasts the appearance of healthy, undamaged plants with the appearance of damaged plants. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;struggling&rdquo; would mean working against difficulties. The text first describes viburnum plants experiencing damage by insects, and the phrase &ldquo;on the other hand&rdquo; then establishes a contrast with that description. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to contrast struggling viburnum plants with those being damaged by insects, because in both cases the plants would be experiencing difficulties. Choice B is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;beneficial&rdquo; would mean producing good or helpful effects. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss how viburnum plants affect other things or suggest that the plants are helpful in some way; rather, it focuses on how viburnum plants are affected by certain conditions. Choice C is incorrect because in this context &ldquo;simple&rdquo; would mean plain or uncomplicated. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss whether certain viburnum plants are complicated or uncomplicated; rather, it focuses on how viburnum plants are affected by certain conditions. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-962-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9f524549-a6cd-490e-b543-daa2c125e718","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$e1e89221$SATQ$, $SATQ$df3063c9-79b5-4510-b41f-913071970103$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a0bb1dce-f9b7-4c1f-953e-573e8cc9cbae$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Frances Hodgson Burnett&rsquo;s 1911 novel <em>The Secret Garden</em>. Mary, a young girl, is outside trying her new jump rope.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The sun was shining and a little wind was blowing&mdash;not a <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>rough</u></span> wind, but one which came in delightful little gusts and brought a fresh scent of newly turned earth with it. She skipped round the fountain garden, and up one walk and down another.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;rough&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0c484494-ccd4-4ea3-93a8-f4b3436367a9","content":"<p>Harsh</p>"},{"id":"8d5c8ce2-593c-4528-9068-64484e180aae","content":"<p>Scratchy</p>"},{"id":"8d22b026-dac3-4dd9-b805-9e2ae3522b35","content":"<p>Basic</p>"},{"id":"4b9c1a09-51eb-4bbc-b0cb-237469044ac3","content":"<p>Vague</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0c484494-ccd4-4ea3-93a8-f4b3436367a9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, "rough" most nearly means harsh, or forceful and unpleasant. The text describes Mary&rsquo;s surroundings as she plays: the sun is out and there&rsquo;s "a little wind." To further illustrate the wind, the narrator contrasts the word "rough" with a description of the wind blowing in "delightful little gusts," suggesting that the wind is not unpleasant or harsh at all.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts "rough" objects, or objects with irregular surfaces, can sometimes be scratchy, rough doesn&rsquo;t mean scratchy in this context. The text explains that the wind is not rough but rather gentle, "delightful little gusts," which suggests that the use of "rough" here is referring to the degree of force of the wind. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the wind wasn&rsquo;t "basic," or simple and uncomplicated. Instead, the text describes the wind as blowing not roughly or harshly but in "delightful little gusts." Choice D is incorrect because the word "vague" means not clearly expressed or seen. Nothing in the text indicates that the wind was barely noticeable to Mary as she played outside, but rather the text states that it was delightful.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-035-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$91c52d4b-7d45-44df-8557-1c043311475c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e1e89221","external_id":"df3063c9-79b5-4510-b41f-913071970103","vaultid":"a0bb1dce-f9b7-4c1f-953e-573e8cc9cbae","uId":"d5300951-73aa-4585-a244-c826df289512","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Frances Hodgson Burnett&rsquo;s 1911 novel <em>The Secret Garden</em>. Mary, a young girl, is outside trying her new jump rope.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The sun was shining and a little wind was blowing&mdash;not a <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>rough</u></span> wind, but one which came in delightful little gusts and brought a fresh scent of newly turned earth with it. She skipped round the fountain garden, and up one walk and down another.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;rough&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0c484494-ccd4-4ea3-93a8-f4b3436367a9","content":"<p>Harsh</p>"},{"id":"8d5c8ce2-593c-4528-9068-64484e180aae","content":"<p>Scratchy</p>"},{"id":"8d22b026-dac3-4dd9-b805-9e2ae3522b35","content":"<p>Basic</p>"},{"id":"4b9c1a09-51eb-4bbc-b0cb-237469044ac3","content":"<p>Vague</p>"}],"keys":["0c484494-ccd4-4ea3-93a8-f4b3436367a9"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, \"rough\" most nearly means harsh, or forceful and unpleasant. The text describes Mary&rsquo;s surroundings as she plays: the sun is out and there&rsquo;s \"a little wind.\" To further illustrate the wind, the narrator contrasts the word \"rough\" with a description of the wind blowing in \"delightful little gusts,\" suggesting that the wind is not unpleasant or harsh at all.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts \"rough\" objects, or objects with irregular surfaces, can sometimes be scratchy, rough doesn&rsquo;t mean scratchy in this context. The text explains that the wind is not rough but rather gentle, \"delightful little gusts,\" which suggests that the use of \"rough\" here is referring to the degree of force of the wind. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest that the wind wasn&rsquo;t \"basic,\" or simple and uncomplicated. Instead, the text describes the wind as blowing not roughly or harshly but in \"delightful little gusts.\" Choice D is incorrect because the word \"vague\" means not clearly expressed or seen. Nothing in the text indicates that the wind was barely noticeable to Mary as she played outside, but rather the text states that it was delightful.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-035-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"91c52d4b-7d45-44df-8557-1c043311475c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959320,"updateDate":1691007959320}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959320, 1691007959320),
    ($SATQ$e23f50b9$SATQ$, $SATQ$0ffd78a5-aea9-40e8-902c-886005749819$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The people of medieval Europe have traditionally been seen as uninterested in cleanliness and hygiene, but modern research has shown that this is largely a myth. According to historian Eleanor Janega, most medieval towns in Europe had at least one public bathhouse, which often offered both full-immersion baths and&mdash;more affordably&mdash;steam baths. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">While such amenities were available mainly to town dwellers</span>, regular bathing in rivers and streams or daily sponge baths at home were common practices throughout medieval Europe.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"741601b8-6583-4dbc-a425-401ef76aeaa6","content":"<p>It asserts that in medieval Europe steam baths were more popular in rural areas than in urban ones.</p>"},{"id":"9964df0c-56ce-4e40-8750-3a068227235f","content":"<p>It describes a limitation of earlier historians&rsquo; studies of medieval European bathing habits.</p>"},{"id":"7896d409-7283-4d37-ac2e-6b10a9afa734","content":"<p>It concedes that not all people in medieval Europe had access to public bathhouses.</p>"},{"id":"19939942-88bb-48af-a4c7-ae2c0dd21701","content":"<p>It explains why Janega decided to study the popularity of public bathhouses in medieval Europe.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7896d409-7283-4d37-ac2e-6b10a9afa734"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively describes the function of the underlined portion. The text discusses the long-standing misconception that people in medieval Europe were uninterested in cleanliness and hygiene. As evidence that this idea is false, the text cites historian Eleanor Janega&rsquo;s assertion that in medieval Europe, towns usually had at least one bathhouse, where people could take immersion baths or steam baths for a fee. The underlined portion then notes that mainly town dwellers had access to these bathhouses. The remainder of the text explains that those who lacked such access were nonetheless able to bathe in outdoor waterways or take sponge baths at home. Therefore, the underlined portion concedes that some people in medieval Europe lacked access to public bathhouses.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The underlined portion establishes that amenities such as steam baths were mainly available to town dwellers, which suggests in turn that steam baths were largely unavailable to people in rural areas. Thus, the distinction made by the underlined portion is not between the popularity of steam baths in towns versus their lack of popularity in rural areas but instead between their presence in towns and absence in rural areas. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text does explain that recent historians have disproved the idea that medieval Europeans rarely bathed, it doesn&rsquo;t attribute that misconception to earlier historians of medieval Europe or suggest that their research was subject to limitations. Moreover, the underlined portion addresses a limitation of life in medieval Europe, not of historical research. Choice D is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t address why historian Eleanor Janega decided to study the popularity of public bathhouses in medieval Europe&mdash;nor does any portion of the text. The text mentions Janega in passing, but it doesn&rsquo;t go into detail about why she decided to study the popularity of public bathhouses in medieval Europe.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e23f50b9","external_id":"0ffd78a5-aea9-40e8-902c-886005749819","vaultid":null,"uId":"db6eb1b9-7dd0-44a8-ad7c-17272f0ab74e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The people of medieval Europe have traditionally been seen as uninterested in cleanliness and hygiene, but modern research has shown that this is largely a myth. According to historian Eleanor Janega, most medieval towns in Europe had at least one public bathhouse, which often offered both full-immersion baths and&mdash;more affordably&mdash;steam baths. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">While such amenities were available mainly to town dwellers</span>, regular bathing in rivers and streams or daily sponge baths at home were common practices throughout medieval Europe.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"741601b8-6583-4dbc-a425-401ef76aeaa6","content":"<p>It asserts that in medieval Europe steam baths were more popular in rural areas than in urban ones.</p>"},{"id":"9964df0c-56ce-4e40-8750-3a068227235f","content":"<p>It describes a limitation of earlier historians&rsquo; studies of medieval European bathing habits.</p>"},{"id":"7896d409-7283-4d37-ac2e-6b10a9afa734","content":"<p>It concedes that not all people in medieval Europe had access to public bathhouses.</p>"},{"id":"19939942-88bb-48af-a4c7-ae2c0dd21701","content":"<p>It explains why Janega decided to study the popularity of public bathhouses in medieval Europe.</p>"}],"keys":["7896d409-7283-4d37-ac2e-6b10a9afa734"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively describes the function of the underlined portion. The text discusses the long-standing misconception that people in medieval Europe were uninterested in cleanliness and hygiene. As evidence that this idea is false, the text cites historian Eleanor Janega&rsquo;s assertion that in medieval Europe, towns usually had at least one bathhouse, where people could take immersion baths or steam baths for a fee. The underlined portion then notes that mainly town dwellers had access to these bathhouses. The remainder of the text explains that those who lacked such access were nonetheless able to bathe in outdoor waterways or take sponge baths at home. Therefore, the underlined portion concedes that some people in medieval Europe lacked access to public bathhouses.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The underlined portion establishes that amenities such as steam baths were mainly available to town dwellers, which suggests in turn that steam baths were largely unavailable to people in rural areas. Thus, the distinction made by the underlined portion is not between the popularity of steam baths in towns versus their lack of popularity in rural areas but instead between their presence in towns and absence in rural areas. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text does explain that recent historians have disproved the idea that medieval Europeans rarely bathed, it doesn&rsquo;t attribute that misconception to earlier historians of medieval Europe or suggest that their research was subject to limitations. Moreover, the underlined portion addresses a limitation of life in medieval Europe, not of historical research. Choice D is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t address why historian Eleanor Janega decided to study the popularity of public bathhouses in medieval Europe&mdash;nor does any portion of the text. The text mentions Janega in passing, but it doesn&rsquo;t go into detail about why she decided to study the popularity of public bathhouses in medieval Europe.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323039,"updateDate":1730147323039}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323039, 1730147323039),
    ($SATQ$e26d23c4$SATQ$, $SATQ$ab82cc07-c157-47a0-b544-992d042c4cb7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Proposals to raise the age at which retirees begin receiving government transfers of funds are generally discussed in terms of the effects on transfer recipients, but Andria Smythe has argued that delaying such transfers could <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> wealth creation among working adults by lengthening the period in which they are providing financial support to their nonworking parents.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8d84cd13-5e7a-4442-9034-3895c55829d9","content":"<p>stymie</p>"},{"id":"f9d2194f-5b1d-4632-94e5-3bf63d71115b","content":"<p>compound</p>"},{"id":"90773700-eccf-4e9e-8835-994d1d14da15","content":"<p>disparage</p>"},{"id":"f6a8f80d-da04-4bdd-be63-22a5c46d8378","content":"<p>outstrip</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8d84cd13-5e7a-4442-9034-3895c55829d9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the consequences of raising the age at which retirees begin receiving government funds. The text indicates that raising the age for these funds is usually discussed in terms of effects on fund recipients but that Andria Smythe is instead considering the effects on working family members who care for retirees. Smythe notes that raising the age for the funds would increase the length of time retirees are dependent on financial assistance from working family members. This is suggested to have an effect on wealth creation for those workers, and most logically, that effect would be disadvantageous. Thus, "stymie," which means to prevent or greatly hinder, is the most logical choice in context.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because in this context, "compound" would most nearly mean multiply or greatly enhance. The text indicates that raising the age at which retirees are eligible for government funds will increase the amount of time retirees are dependent on working family members for financial support. This would likely have a negative rather than a positive effect on wealth creation. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "disparage" would most nearly mean criticize or defame. Nothing in the text suggests that raising the age at which retirees are eligible for government funds would defame wealth creation among working adults. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "outstrip" would most nearly mean to exceed, and nothing in the text indicates that the financial support provided to retirees would exceed the amount of wealth these workers can create. The text does suggest that workers providing funds to retirees works against those workers&rsquo; wealth accumulation, but not that the support to retirees exceeds the workers&rsquo; accumulated wealth.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e26d23c4","external_id":"ab82cc07-c157-47a0-b544-992d042c4cb7","vaultid":null,"uId":"391df5fb-481e-49b0-9631-38924be71838","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Proposals to raise the age at which retirees begin receiving government transfers of funds are generally discussed in terms of the effects on transfer recipients, but Andria Smythe has argued that delaying such transfers could <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> wealth creation among working adults by lengthening the period in which they are providing financial support to their nonworking parents.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8d84cd13-5e7a-4442-9034-3895c55829d9","content":"<p>stymie</p>"},{"id":"f9d2194f-5b1d-4632-94e5-3bf63d71115b","content":"<p>compound</p>"},{"id":"90773700-eccf-4e9e-8835-994d1d14da15","content":"<p>disparage</p>"},{"id":"f6a8f80d-da04-4bdd-be63-22a5c46d8378","content":"<p>outstrip</p>"}],"keys":["8d84cd13-5e7a-4442-9034-3895c55829d9"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the consequences of raising the age at which retirees begin receiving government funds. The text indicates that raising the age for these funds is usually discussed in terms of effects on fund recipients but that Andria Smythe is instead considering the effects on working family members who care for retirees. Smythe notes that raising the age for the funds would increase the length of time retirees are dependent on financial assistance from working family members. This is suggested to have an effect on wealth creation for those workers, and most logically, that effect would be disadvantageous. Thus, \"stymie,\" which means to prevent or greatly hinder, is the most logical choice in context.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because in this context, \"compound\" would most nearly mean multiply or greatly enhance. The text indicates that raising the age at which retirees are eligible for government funds will increase the amount of time retirees are dependent on working family members for financial support. This would likely have a negative rather than a positive effect on wealth creation. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"disparage\" would most nearly mean criticize or defame. Nothing in the text suggests that raising the age at which retirees are eligible for government funds would defame wealth creation among working adults. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, \"outstrip\" would most nearly mean to exceed, and nothing in the text indicates that the financial support provided to retirees would exceed the amount of wealth these workers can create. The text does suggest that workers providing funds to retirees works against those workers&rsquo; wealth accumulation, but not that the support to retirees exceeds the workers&rsquo; accumulated wealth.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$e35d481c$SATQ$, $SATQ$bc429b96-1413-4cd8-89c4-e3801bcd22b4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1e4d32df-cc76-4615-954e-509fbc8397a3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Some economic historians <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> that late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century households in the United States experienced an economy of scale when it came to food purchases&mdash;they assumed that large households spent less on food per person than did small households. Economist Trevon Logan showed, however, that a close look at the available data disproves this supposition.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5bd13f6d-ce50-48bd-906c-07eb6ff0e9b6","content":"<p>surmised</p>"},{"id":"34ebb595-8996-42f7-b929-0a0b020eee7f","content":"<p>contrived</p>"},{"id":"62337d77-3a60-4cb7-88a1-ff37a24fd06c","content":"<p>questioned</p>"},{"id":"8676f2a0-8f3f-4dea-bdca-0ee8658b9357","content":"<p>regretted</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5bd13f6d-ce50-48bd-906c-07eb6ff0e9b6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century household food purchases. In this context, &ldquo;surmised&rdquo; means formed an idea or assumption with little evidence. The text explains that certain economic historians &ldquo;assumed&rdquo; that large and small households spent different amounts on food per person, but that another economist found this supposition to be false based on evidence from available data. This context suggests that the economic historians made an incorrect assumption without enough consideration of evidence. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;contrived&rdquo; would mean brought about or created through trickery. Nothing in the text suggests that the economic historians were deliberately trying to trick people with a claim about food purchasing behaviors in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century households; the text simply suggests that they made an assumption about those behaviors that another historian believes isn&rsquo;t supported by the available data.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that it&rsquo;s Logan and not the economic historians who &ldquo;questioned,&rdquo; or doubted, the assumption that large and small households in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries spent different amounts on food per person; the economic historians are the ones who made that assumption to begin with. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that some economic historians &ldquo;regretted,&rdquo; or felt sad or remorseful about, the food purchasing behaviors of late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century households. The text focuses on the idea that the economic historians made an assumption about those behaviors that may not be supported by available data, not on the historians&rsquo; emotional response to what households did in the past. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-912-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1769ea23-fa02-4562-a175-55e4fb40aa55$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e35d481c","external_id":"bc429b96-1413-4cd8-89c4-e3801bcd22b4","vaultid":"1e4d32df-cc76-4615-954e-509fbc8397a3","uId":"070cc628-5e22-4f16-9fc3-47500b504daa","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Some economic historians <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> that late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century households in the United States experienced an economy of scale when it came to food purchases&mdash;they assumed that large households spent less on food per person than did small households. Economist Trevon Logan showed, however, that a close look at the available data disproves this supposition.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5bd13f6d-ce50-48bd-906c-07eb6ff0e9b6","content":"<p>surmised</p>"},{"id":"34ebb595-8996-42f7-b929-0a0b020eee7f","content":"<p>contrived</p>"},{"id":"62337d77-3a60-4cb7-88a1-ff37a24fd06c","content":"<p>questioned</p>"},{"id":"8676f2a0-8f3f-4dea-bdca-0ee8658b9357","content":"<p>regretted</p>"}],"keys":["5bd13f6d-ce50-48bd-906c-07eb6ff0e9b6"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century household food purchases. In this context, &ldquo;surmised&rdquo; means formed an idea or assumption with little evidence. The text explains that certain economic historians &ldquo;assumed&rdquo; that large and small households spent different amounts on food per person, but that another economist found this supposition to be false based on evidence from available data. This context suggests that the economic historians made an incorrect assumption without enough consideration of evidence. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. In this context, &ldquo;contrived&rdquo; would mean brought about or created through trickery. Nothing in the text suggests that the economic historians were deliberately trying to trick people with a claim about food purchasing behaviors in late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century households; the text simply suggests that they made an assumption about those behaviors that another historian believes isn&rsquo;t supported by the available data.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that it&rsquo;s Logan and not the economic historians who &ldquo;questioned,&rdquo; or doubted, the assumption that large and small households in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries spent different amounts on food per person; the economic historians are the ones who made that assumption to begin with. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that some economic historians &ldquo;regretted,&rdquo; or felt sad or remorseful about, the food purchasing behaviors of late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century households. The text focuses on the idea that the economic historians made an assumption about those behaviors that may not be supported by available data, not on the historians&rsquo; emotional response to what households did in the past. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-912-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1769ea23-fa02-4562-a175-55e4fb40aa55","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959354,"updateDate":1691007959354}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959354, 1691007959354),
    ($SATQ$e37b9e34$SATQ$, $SATQ$24e1b704-452c-4aee-9a4a-ed63134e1c83$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$041af717-c0cd-4e96-a357-c28d3fdd100f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Some researchers believe that the genes that enable groundhogs and certain other mammals to hibernate through the winter by slowing their breathing and heart rates and lowering their body temperature may be <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in humans: present yet having essentially no effect on our bodily processes.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"473ea2a8-8f1a-4f71-aa26-ef7cb3e829e7","content":"<p>decisive</p>"},{"id":"a33842ed-6270-45c8-b5cf-b4c1f7debbcd","content":"<p>lacking</p>"},{"id":"733896ef-24ea-4936-a348-8011e9a383c9","content":"<p>variable</p>"},{"id":"3e502a42-074a-4b99-991e-3c262af0abc9","content":"<p>dormant</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3e502a42-074a-4b99-991e-3c262af0abc9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion about genes related to hibernation. In this context, &ldquo;dormant&rdquo; means inactive. The text explains that the same genes that enable certain nonhuman mammal species to hibernate during the winter by altering their bodily processes are also found in our species but have &ldquo;essentially no effect&rdquo; on humans&rsquo; bodily processes. In other words, these genes don&rsquo;t function in humans. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;decisive&rdquo; means has the power to affect the outcome of something, but the text states that genes related to hibernation are instead inactive in humans&mdash;that is, the genes don&rsquo;t affect humans&rsquo; bodily processes, although they are present in their bodies.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;lacking&rdquo; means missing, but the text states that the genes are present in humans, though inactive. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;variable&rdquo; means characterized by the potential to change, but the text indicates that these genes don&rsquo;t change in their effect on humans&rsquo; bodily processes; instead, the genes are consistently inactive in humans. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-915-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7ae8fb2e-9cc3-4490-abe5-cede714645d4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e37b9e34","external_id":"24e1b704-452c-4aee-9a4a-ed63134e1c83","vaultid":"041af717-c0cd-4e96-a357-c28d3fdd100f","uId":"aa8b0296-f381-4835-822f-248d14b47943","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Some researchers believe that the genes that enable groundhogs and certain other mammals to hibernate through the winter by slowing their breathing and heart rates and lowering their body temperature may be <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in humans: present yet having essentially no effect on our bodily processes.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"473ea2a8-8f1a-4f71-aa26-ef7cb3e829e7","content":"<p>decisive</p>"},{"id":"a33842ed-6270-45c8-b5cf-b4c1f7debbcd","content":"<p>lacking</p>"},{"id":"733896ef-24ea-4936-a348-8011e9a383c9","content":"<p>variable</p>"},{"id":"3e502a42-074a-4b99-991e-3c262af0abc9","content":"<p>dormant</p>"}],"keys":["3e502a42-074a-4b99-991e-3c262af0abc9"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion about genes related to hibernation. In this context, &ldquo;dormant&rdquo; means inactive. The text explains that the same genes that enable certain nonhuman mammal species to hibernate during the winter by altering their bodily processes are also found in our species but have &ldquo;essentially no effect&rdquo; on humans&rsquo; bodily processes. In other words, these genes don&rsquo;t function in humans. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;decisive&rdquo; means has the power to affect the outcome of something, but the text states that genes related to hibernation are instead inactive in humans&mdash;that is, the genes don&rsquo;t affect humans&rsquo; bodily processes, although they are present in their bodies.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because in this context, &ldquo;lacking&rdquo; means missing, but the text states that the genes are present in humans, though inactive. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;variable&rdquo; means characterized by the potential to change, but the text indicates that these genes don&rsquo;t change in their effect on humans&rsquo; bodily processes; instead, the genes are consistently inactive in humans. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-915-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7ae8fb2e-9cc3-4490-abe5-cede714645d4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959354,"updateDate":1691007959354}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959354, 1691007959354),
    ($SATQ$e386a11d$SATQ$, $SATQ$863bb152-6133-4984-90ca-8ef258efa21c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> is an important work of <span lang="ja"><em>Nihonga</em></span>, or classical Japanese painting. Unlike Kuroda Seiki, who adopted traditional European methods such as painting with oil on canvas, Hishida Shunsō <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> traditional Japanese approaches. For instance, Hishida produced <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> by applying color pigments to a silk scroll.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1d54ec28-cc3d-4c59-acdc-483744f88b5f","content":"<p>distrusted</p>"},{"id":"6b42a849-20df-4752-bbe0-e34199b0f489","content":"<p>embraced</p>"},{"id":"44a8c010-115f-4e9e-8265-d49a5c93e7ec","content":"<p>released</p>"},{"id":"4c0b49d0-fe4c-4291-98ba-a77303ceb5c5","content":"<p>overlooked</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6b42a849-20df-4752-bbe0-e34199b0f489"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Hishida Shunsō&rsquo;s painting <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em>. In this context, "embraced" means accepted or adopted readily. The text begins by identifying <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> as an example of <em>Nihonga</em>, or classical (that is, traditional) Japanese painting. The text continues by drawing a contrast between Hishida&rsquo;s work and Kuroda Seiki&rsquo;s work, which differs from that of Hishida in its use of the traditional European method of using oil paints on canvas rather than using traditional Japanese approaches. The text then concludes with an example of one such traditional Japanese painting technique exemplified by Hishida&rsquo;s <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em>&mdash;applications of color pigments to a silk scroll&mdash;to further develop the contrast between the two artists&rsquo; approaches. This context supports the idea that Hishida adopted, or embraced, traditional Japanese approaches.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context "distrusted" would mean that Hishida was skeptical of or lacked confidence in traditional Japanese approaches to painting. The text begins by stating that Hishida&rsquo;s painting <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> is an example of <em>Nihonga</em>, or traditional Japanese painting, and then cites Hishida&rsquo;s application of color pigments to a silk scroll as an instance of a traditional Japanese painting technique. It therefore wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Hishida&rsquo;s use of traditional Japanese painting techniques indicates his distrust, or skepticism, of them. Rather, it suggests that he embraced those techniques. Choice C is incorrect because saying that Hishida "released" traditional Japanese approaches to painting in this context would mean that he freed them from any restrictions. In using Hishida&rsquo;s application of color pigments to a silk scroll in <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> as an example of a traditional Japanese approach to painting found in <em>Nihonga</em>, the text strongly implies that Hishida followed these approaches, not that he removed them from any restrictions. Choice D is incorrect because in this context "overlooked" would mean ignored or failed to notice. The text establishes that Hishida&rsquo;s painting <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> is a notable work of <em>Nihonga</em>, or classical Japanese painting, that features the traditional technique of applying color pigments to a silk scroll. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Hishida&rsquo;s use of a traditional Japanese approach to painting indicates that Hishida failed to notice those approaches.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e386a11d","external_id":"863bb152-6133-4984-90ca-8ef258efa21c","vaultid":null,"uId":"0b3d6def-4740-46b5-b2d8-b304f4b77f45","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> is an important work of <span lang=\"ja\"><em>Nihonga</em></span>, or classical Japanese painting. Unlike Kuroda Seiki, who adopted traditional European methods such as painting with oil on canvas, Hishida Shunsō <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> traditional Japanese approaches. For instance, Hishida produced <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> by applying color pigments to a silk scroll.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1d54ec28-cc3d-4c59-acdc-483744f88b5f","content":"<p>distrusted</p>"},{"id":"6b42a849-20df-4752-bbe0-e34199b0f489","content":"<p>embraced</p>"},{"id":"44a8c010-115f-4e9e-8265-d49a5c93e7ec","content":"<p>released</p>"},{"id":"4c0b49d0-fe4c-4291-98ba-a77303ceb5c5","content":"<p>overlooked</p>"}],"keys":["6b42a849-20df-4752-bbe0-e34199b0f489"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Hishida Shunsō&rsquo;s painting <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em>. In this context, \"embraced\" means accepted or adopted readily. The text begins by identifying <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> as an example of <em>Nihonga</em>, or classical (that is, traditional) Japanese painting. The text continues by drawing a contrast between Hishida&rsquo;s work and Kuroda Seiki&rsquo;s work, which differs from that of Hishida in its use of the traditional European method of using oil paints on canvas rather than using traditional Japanese approaches. The text then concludes with an example of one such traditional Japanese painting technique exemplified by Hishida&rsquo;s <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em>&mdash;applications of color pigments to a silk scroll&mdash;to further develop the contrast between the two artists&rsquo; approaches. This context supports the idea that Hishida adopted, or embraced, traditional Japanese approaches.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context \"distrusted\" would mean that Hishida was skeptical of or lacked confidence in traditional Japanese approaches to painting. The text begins by stating that Hishida&rsquo;s painting <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> is an example of <em>Nihonga</em>, or traditional Japanese painting, and then cites Hishida&rsquo;s application of color pigments to a silk scroll as an instance of a traditional Japanese painting technique. It therefore wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Hishida&rsquo;s use of traditional Japanese painting techniques indicates his distrust, or skepticism, of them. Rather, it suggests that he embraced those techniques. Choice C is incorrect because saying that Hishida \"released\" traditional Japanese approaches to painting in this context would mean that he freed them from any restrictions. In using Hishida&rsquo;s application of color pigments to a silk scroll in <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> as an example of a traditional Japanese approach to painting found in <em>Nihonga</em>, the text strongly implies that Hishida followed these approaches, not that he removed them from any restrictions. Choice D is incorrect because in this context \"overlooked\" would mean ignored or failed to notice. The text establishes that Hishida&rsquo;s painting <em>Cat and Plum Blossoms</em> is a notable work of <em>Nihonga</em>, or classical Japanese painting, that features the traditional technique of applying color pigments to a silk scroll. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Hishida&rsquo;s use of a traditional Japanese approach to painting indicates that Hishida failed to notice those approaches.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061189,"updateDate":1755117061189}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061189, 1755117061189),
    ($SATQ$e3f05561$SATQ$, $SATQ$9f4b47ff-c0cb-4a47-be10-410a57a2160d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f2755f3c-4b96-4ce4-bca3-e445b5c9d093$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the 1970s, video cameras became increasingly affordable for ordinary consumers and gave Ulysses Jenkins and other artists capabilities that were previously unavailable except to television broadcasters. Jenkins recognized and took full advantage of this <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> access to powerful technology to create groundbreaking works of video art, such as <em>Mass of Images</em> (1978).</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"506d6115-4f43-4263-a24a-3ea582a765c0","content":"<p>newfound</p>"},{"id":"2bdf9252-421a-4bca-8774-fc961115505d","content":"<p>delicate</p>"},{"id":"f37ffdaf-5a74-4fc4-be8e-0c6906f43bbf","content":"<p>inevitable</p>"},{"id":"577db509-4009-409f-a9f1-fc2823e5bbad","content":"<p>habitual</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["506d6115-4f43-4263-a24a-3ea582a765c0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Ulysses Jenkins&rsquo;s video art. As used in this context, &ldquo;newfound&rdquo; means recently discovered or established. The text indicates that in the 1970s, video cameras became cheaper and therefore more widely available than they had been in the past. The text goes on to say that this development provided Jenkins and other artists with capabilities that they previously didn&rsquo;t have. As a result, Jenkins began producing groundbreaking works of video art. This context supports the idea that Jenkins took advantage of newfound access to video cameras. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;delicate&rdquo; means fine in texture or structure or easily broken, neither of which would make sense in this context. The text doesn&rsquo;t focus on describing what video art looks like or whether it&rsquo;s breakable. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;inevitable&rdquo; means impossible to avoid, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the likelihood of artists, or anyone else, gaining access to video cameras. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;habitual&rdquo; means doing something regularly or repeatedly. Although the text does suggest that Jenkins created multiple pieces of video art, its focus is on the fact that video cameras had only just become widely available to artists in the 1970s. Jenkins&rsquo;s ability to take advantage of video cameras to make art was therefore newfound, not habitual, at the time. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-046-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f4c1360b-1314-46da-ba19-bc2a69ca48ea$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e3f05561","external_id":"9f4b47ff-c0cb-4a47-be10-410a57a2160d","vaultid":"f2755f3c-4b96-4ce4-bca3-e445b5c9d093","uId":"1ef243c7-a41f-48b5-bb96-2836e3e165e7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the 1970s, video cameras became increasingly affordable for ordinary consumers and gave Ulysses Jenkins and other artists capabilities that were previously unavailable except to television broadcasters. Jenkins recognized and took full advantage of this <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> access to powerful technology to create groundbreaking works of video art, such as <em>Mass of Images</em> (1978).</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"506d6115-4f43-4263-a24a-3ea582a765c0","content":"<p>newfound</p>"},{"id":"2bdf9252-421a-4bca-8774-fc961115505d","content":"<p>delicate</p>"},{"id":"f37ffdaf-5a74-4fc4-be8e-0c6906f43bbf","content":"<p>inevitable</p>"},{"id":"577db509-4009-409f-a9f1-fc2823e5bbad","content":"<p>habitual</p>"}],"keys":["506d6115-4f43-4263-a24a-3ea582a765c0"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Ulysses Jenkins&rsquo;s video art. As used in this context, &ldquo;newfound&rdquo; means recently discovered or established. The text indicates that in the 1970s, video cameras became cheaper and therefore more widely available than they had been in the past. The text goes on to say that this development provided Jenkins and other artists with capabilities that they previously didn&rsquo;t have. As a result, Jenkins began producing groundbreaking works of video art. This context supports the idea that Jenkins took advantage of newfound access to video cameras. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;delicate&rdquo; means fine in texture or structure or easily broken, neither of which would make sense in this context. The text doesn&rsquo;t focus on describing what video art looks like or whether it&rsquo;s breakable. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;inevitable&rdquo; means impossible to avoid, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the likelihood of artists, or anyone else, gaining access to video cameras. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;habitual&rdquo; means doing something regularly or repeatedly. Although the text does suggest that Jenkins created multiple pieces of video art, its focus is on the fact that video cameras had only just become widely available to artists in the 1970s. Jenkins&rsquo;s ability to take advantage of video cameras to make art was therefore newfound, not habitual, at the time. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-046-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f4c1360b-1314-46da-ba19-bc2a69ca48ea","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959323,"updateDate":1691007959323}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959323, 1691007959323),
    ($SATQ$e3ffb854$SATQ$, $SATQ$ff1f5493-83bc-4145-abcd-5a553c514505$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from John Matheus&rsquo;s 1925 short story &ldquo;Fog.&rdquo;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The fog extended its tentacles over city and river, gradually obliterating traces of familiar landscapes. At five-thirty the old Panhandle bridge, <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>supported</u></span> by massive sandstone pillars, stalwart, as when erected fifty years before to serve a generation now passed behind the portals of life, had become a spectral outline against the sky.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;supported&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"64e3e1f2-241e-4e7a-862d-6add9a3a144a","content":"<p>Held up</p>"},{"id":"e3650117-b387-4cbf-b3c0-006eb5260d6c","content":"<p>Encouraged</p>"},{"id":"b6a9fa42-31f6-4f13-8acd-b636ed66d16c","content":"<p>Improved on</p>"},{"id":"e7818440-c0f6-489c-82a7-7c44019db08a","content":"<p>Defended</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["64e3e1f2-241e-4e7a-862d-6add9a3a144a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, "supported" most nearly means held up or sustained physically. The sentence describes the physical structure of the Panhandle bridge, stating it was "supported by massive sandstone pillars." This clearly refers to the structural relationship between the bridge and its pillars&mdash;the pillars physically hold up the bridge and keep it standing. The text further emphasizes the physical nature of this support by describing the pillars as "stalwart" (strong and sturdy) and noting they&rsquo;ve been performing this function for fifty years.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts "supported" can mean "encouraged," the sandstone pillars and bridge described in the text are all inanimate objects that can&rsquo;t provide or receive encouragement. The text clearly describes a structural relationship between the elements of the bridge, not an emotional one. Choice C is incorrect. Although in some contexts "supported" has a meaning similar to "improved on," the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the sandstone pillars have enhanced or made the bridge better; rather, they simply provide the necessary physical foundation to keep the bridge standing. Thus, the sentence describes the structural elements of the bridge, not improvements made to it. Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts "supported" can mean "defended," the text doesn&rsquo;t present a situation in which the sandstone pillars are defending the bridge from a threat. Instead, the description focuses on the physical structure of the bridge, in which the pillars hold the bridge up and ensure its stability.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e3ffb854","external_id":"ff1f5493-83bc-4145-abcd-5a553c514505","vaultid":null,"uId":"54361a8e-a8ea-4415-bf70-157e80219b28","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from John Matheus&rsquo;s 1925 short story &ldquo;Fog.&rdquo;</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The fog extended its tentacles over city and river, gradually obliterating traces of familiar landscapes. At five-thirty the old Panhandle bridge, <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>supported</u></span> by massive sandstone pillars, stalwart, as when erected fifty years before to serve a generation now passed behind the portals of life, had become a spectral outline against the sky.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;supported&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"64e3e1f2-241e-4e7a-862d-6add9a3a144a","content":"<p>Held up</p>"},{"id":"e3650117-b387-4cbf-b3c0-006eb5260d6c","content":"<p>Encouraged</p>"},{"id":"b6a9fa42-31f6-4f13-8acd-b636ed66d16c","content":"<p>Improved on</p>"},{"id":"e7818440-c0f6-489c-82a7-7c44019db08a","content":"<p>Defended</p>"}],"keys":["64e3e1f2-241e-4e7a-862d-6add9a3a144a"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, \"supported\" most nearly means held up or sustained physically. The sentence describes the physical structure of the Panhandle bridge, stating it was \"supported by massive sandstone pillars.\" This clearly refers to the structural relationship between the bridge and its pillars&mdash;the pillars physically hold up the bridge and keep it standing. The text further emphasizes the physical nature of this support by describing the pillars as \"stalwart\" (strong and sturdy) and noting they&rsquo;ve been performing this function for fifty years.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts \"supported\" can mean \"encouraged,\" the sandstone pillars and bridge described in the text are all inanimate objects that can&rsquo;t provide or receive encouragement. The text clearly describes a structural relationship between the elements of the bridge, not an emotional one. Choice C is incorrect. Although in some contexts \"supported\" has a meaning similar to \"improved on,\" the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the sandstone pillars have enhanced or made the bridge better; rather, they simply provide the necessary physical foundation to keep the bridge standing. Thus, the sentence describes the structural elements of the bridge, not improvements made to it. Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts \"supported\" can mean \"defended,\" the text doesn&rsquo;t present a situation in which the sandstone pillars are defending the bridge from a threat. Instead, the description focuses on the physical structure of the bridge, in which the pillars hold the bridge up and ensure its stability.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061193,"updateDate":1755117061193}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061193, 1755117061193),
    ($SATQ$e41dfaab$SATQ$, $SATQ$50dae82d-91e1-489c-ab31-8af664bc9707$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$0b273599-157e-4ada-bcae-9dd0d32ae59a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1929 the<em> Atlantic Monthly </em>published several articles based on newly discovered letters allegedly exchanged between President Abraham Lincoln and a woman named Ann Rutledge. Historians were unable to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the authenticity of the letters, however, and quickly dismissed them as a hoax.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1844afeb-f4b0-409b-b934-47dfabd8f04f","content":"<p>validate</p>"},{"id":"49eef0ad-0375-419e-b743-e1445753617c","content":"<p>interpret</p>"},{"id":"6ae92e72-db02-4120-bae7-646de0c758e5","content":"<p>relate</p>"},{"id":"4169d29b-f3b2-4c6b-9b5f-3ed83ceffd22","content":"<p>accommodate</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1844afeb-f4b0-409b-b934-47dfabd8f04f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of letters allegedly exchanged between President Lincoln and Rutledge. In this context, &ldquo;validate&rdquo; means to confirm that something is real or correct. According to the text, it was alleged, or claimed, that the newly discovered letters had been written by Lincoln and Rutledge. The text also indicates that historians ultimately decided the letters were a hoax, or fraudulent. This context suggests that the historians couldn&rsquo;t confirm that the letters were authentic. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The text focuses on the authenticity of the letters, which were claimed to have been written by Lincoln and Rutledge and were then quickly dismissed as fraudulent by historians. Rather than conveying that the historians simply weren&rsquo;t able to &ldquo;interpret,&rdquo; or explain in an understandable way, the letters&rsquo; authenticity, the text suggests that the historians decided the letters lacked authenticity altogether. Choice C is incorrect. The text states that the historians quickly dismissed the letters claimed to have been written by Lincoln and Rutledge as fraudulent; this suggests that rather than being unable to &ldquo;relate,&rdquo; or tell others about, the letters&rsquo; authenticity, the historians were able to share what they&rsquo;d decided about the letters. Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to suggest that the historians couldn&rsquo;t &ldquo;accommodate,&rdquo; or give consideration to, the authenticity of the letters claimed to have been written by Lincoln and Rutledge; the text states that the historians decided that the letters were fraudulent, which indicates that they <em>did </em>consider whether the letters were authentic. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-965-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$334effdf-eb20-498d-8d2a-d10bf80dd2c4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e41dfaab","external_id":"50dae82d-91e1-489c-ab31-8af664bc9707","vaultid":"0b273599-157e-4ada-bcae-9dd0d32ae59a","uId":"2d5c7023-43e6-4ef2-85e5-2a45eb3004d4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1929 the<em> Atlantic Monthly </em>published several articles based on newly discovered letters allegedly exchanged between President Abraham Lincoln and a woman named Ann Rutledge. Historians were unable to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the authenticity of the letters, however, and quickly dismissed them as a hoax.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1844afeb-f4b0-409b-b934-47dfabd8f04f","content":"<p>validate</p>"},{"id":"49eef0ad-0375-419e-b743-e1445753617c","content":"<p>interpret</p>"},{"id":"6ae92e72-db02-4120-bae7-646de0c758e5","content":"<p>relate</p>"},{"id":"4169d29b-f3b2-4c6b-9b5f-3ed83ceffd22","content":"<p>accommodate</p>"}],"keys":["1844afeb-f4b0-409b-b934-47dfabd8f04f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of letters allegedly exchanged between President Lincoln and Rutledge. In this context, &ldquo;validate&rdquo; means to confirm that something is real or correct. According to the text, it was alleged, or claimed, that the newly discovered letters had been written by Lincoln and Rutledge. The text also indicates that historians ultimately decided the letters were a hoax, or fraudulent. This context suggests that the historians couldn&rsquo;t confirm that the letters were authentic. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The text focuses on the authenticity of the letters, which were claimed to have been written by Lincoln and Rutledge and were then quickly dismissed as fraudulent by historians. Rather than conveying that the historians simply weren&rsquo;t able to &ldquo;interpret,&rdquo; or explain in an understandable way, the letters&rsquo; authenticity, the text suggests that the historians decided the letters lacked authenticity altogether. Choice C is incorrect. The text states that the historians quickly dismissed the letters claimed to have been written by Lincoln and Rutledge as fraudulent; this suggests that rather than being unable to &ldquo;relate,&rdquo; or tell others about, the letters&rsquo; authenticity, the historians were able to share what they&rsquo;d decided about the letters. Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to suggest that the historians couldn&rsquo;t &ldquo;accommodate,&rdquo; or give consideration to, the authenticity of the letters claimed to have been written by Lincoln and Rutledge; the text states that the historians decided that the letters were fraudulent, which indicates that they <em>did </em>consider whether the letters were authentic. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-965-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"334effdf-eb20-498d-8d2a-d10bf80dd2c4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$e459076b$SATQ$, $SATQ$dbc2545e-b96c-4d5d-96ed-0f7e1a1c373d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$04ec0a10-b4ed-420b-9e5f-2aec47d8a24b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from George Eliot&rsquo;s 1871&ndash;72 novel <em>Middlemarch</em>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[Mr. Brooke] had travelled in his younger years, and was held in this part of the country to have <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>contracted</u></span> a too rambling habit of mind. Mr. Brooke&rsquo;s conclusions were as difficult to predict as the weather.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;contracted&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2d31d220-87b3-4324-948c-e334ed715b4c","content":"<p>Restricted</p>"},{"id":"f461f3c8-28d5-4051-9fd5-17995e977ad7","content":"<p>Described</p>"},{"id":"ffc74d0b-0e2c-4541-a481-ee82438045eb","content":"<p>Developed</p>"},{"id":"090a32b2-0e10-49bf-89aa-9070e12fc142","content":"<p>Settled</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ffc74d0b-0e2c-4541-a481-ee82438045eb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;contracted&rdquo; most nearly means developed. The text explains that Mr. Brooke has a &ldquo;too rambling habit of mind,&rdquo; which the text likens to a disease, saying he is thought to have contracted it. To contract a disease means to acquire or develop a disease. In other words, the text indicates that Mr. Brooke is believed to have acquired, or developed, the habit of mind described in the text. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although &ldquo;contracted&rdquo; can mean limited or restricted in some contexts, here Mr. Brooke is said to draw unpredictable conclusions, suggesting that he exhibits this &ldquo;too rambling habit of mind,&rdquo; not that it has been somehow limited or restricted. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text describes Mr. Brooke&rsquo;s habit of mind, nothing suggests that those are his descriptions or, indeed, that he described his habit of mind at all. Choice D is incorrect because settled means calmed or mitigated, but here Mr. Brooke is said to draw unpredictable conclusions, suggesting that he exhibits this &ldquo;too rambling habit of mind,&rdquo; not that it has been somehow calmed or mitigated. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$5d48414c-ecc4-49c7-a08f-d57e8a1e3d5b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e459076b","external_id":"dbc2545e-b96c-4d5d-96ed-0f7e1a1c373d","vaultid":"04ec0a10-b4ed-420b-9e5f-2aec47d8a24b","uId":"1538320c-40e6-40a1-99fc-7733e763e1ef","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from George Eliot&rsquo;s 1871&ndash;72 novel <em>Middlemarch</em>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[Mr. Brooke] had travelled in his younger years, and was held in this part of the country to have <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>contracted</u></span> a too rambling habit of mind. Mr. Brooke&rsquo;s conclusions were as difficult to predict as the weather.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;contracted&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2d31d220-87b3-4324-948c-e334ed715b4c","content":"<p>Restricted</p>"},{"id":"f461f3c8-28d5-4051-9fd5-17995e977ad7","content":"<p>Described</p>"},{"id":"ffc74d0b-0e2c-4541-a481-ee82438045eb","content":"<p>Developed</p>"},{"id":"090a32b2-0e10-49bf-89aa-9070e12fc142","content":"<p>Settled</p>"}],"keys":["ffc74d0b-0e2c-4541-a481-ee82438045eb"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;contracted&rdquo; most nearly means developed. The text explains that Mr. Brooke has a &ldquo;too rambling habit of mind,&rdquo; which the text likens to a disease, saying he is thought to have contracted it. To contract a disease means to acquire or develop a disease. In other words, the text indicates that Mr. Brooke is believed to have acquired, or developed, the habit of mind described in the text. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although &ldquo;contracted&rdquo; can mean limited or restricted in some contexts, here Mr. Brooke is said to draw unpredictable conclusions, suggesting that he exhibits this &ldquo;too rambling habit of mind,&rdquo; not that it has been somehow limited or restricted. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text describes Mr. Brooke&rsquo;s habit of mind, nothing suggests that those are his descriptions or, indeed, that he described his habit of mind at all. Choice D is incorrect because settled means calmed or mitigated, but here Mr. Brooke is said to draw unpredictable conclusions, suggesting that he exhibits this &ldquo;too rambling habit of mind,&rdquo; not that it has been somehow calmed or mitigated. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"5d48414c-ecc4-49c7-a08f-d57e8a1e3d5b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$e4e2aeb3$SATQ$, $SATQ$dc9f8834-4a33-4df5-95e4-1900e4f12d32$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2b590194-86e9-46cd-b508-2c478e69f9e4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Text 1</p>
<p>Like the work of Ralph Ellison before her, Toni Morrison&rsquo;s novels feature scenes in which characters deliver sermons of such length and verbal dexterity that for a time, the text exchanges the formal parameters of fiction for those of oral literature. Given the many other echoes of Ellison in Morrison&rsquo;s novels, both in structure and prose style, <span style="text-decoration: underline;">these scenes suggest Ellison&rsquo;s direct influence on Morrison.</span></p>
<p>Text 2</p>
<p>In their destabilizing effect on literary form, the sermons in Morrison&rsquo;s works recall those in Ellison&rsquo;s. Yet literature by Black Americans abounds in moments where interpolated speech erodes the division between oral and written forms that literature in English has traditionally observed. Morrison&rsquo;s use of the sermon is attributable not only to the influence of Ellison but also to a community-wide strategy of resistance to externally imposed literary conventions.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely characterize the underlined claim in Text 1?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8fa1c8bd-cdea-4eff-802e-53b786079cbb","content":"<p>As failing to consider Ellison&rsquo;s and Morrison&rsquo;s equivalent uses of the sermon within the wider cultural context in which they wrote</p>"},{"id":"fb4a5e70-7426-433d-a640-65b1f91b1147","content":"<p>As misunderstanding the function of sermons in novels by Black American writers other than Ellison and Morrison</p>"},{"id":"85dfbf48-16ce-4e6c-9128-1d7c69f21d43","content":"<p>As disregarding points of structural and stylistic divergence between the works of Ellison and those of Morrison</p>"},{"id":"610d33c5-5dbb-48c7-bf86-338d61beba4f","content":"<p>As being indebted to the tradition of resisting literary conventions that privilege written forms, such as novels, over sermons and other oral forms</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8fa1c8bd-cdea-4eff-802e-53b786079cbb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The author of Text 2 argues that Morrison&rsquo;s use of the sermon is not only influenced by Ellison, but also by a &ldquo;community-wide strategy of resistance&rdquo; to literary conventions practiced by Black American authors. Ellison, Text 2 alleges, is only one of many influences on Morrison. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Neither text specifically mentions sermons in works by authors other than Morrison or Ellison, only a tendency towards eroding &ldquo;the division between oral and written forms&rdquo; among Black American writers. Choice C is incorrect. Both texts describe similarities between the works of Ellison and Morrison, and neither points out instances of divergence. Text 2 simply suggests that Morrison was influenced by more than just Ellison. Choice D is incorrect. While Text 2 does discuss Morrison&rsquo;s resistance to certain literary conventions, it&rsquo;s unclear what it would mean for the underlined claim to be &ldquo;indebted&rdquo; to that tradition. This choice recycles language from the text, but not in a way that makes any coherent point. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-CTC-H005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$779a3333-321f-4696-8583-c37d9735704b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e4e2aeb3","external_id":"dc9f8834-4a33-4df5-95e4-1900e4f12d32","vaultid":"2b590194-86e9-46cd-b508-2c478e69f9e4","uId":"a042cb3b-0f7e-4d1d-8932-8a72b9cb469f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Text 1</p>\n<p>Like the work of Ralph Ellison before her, Toni Morrison&rsquo;s novels feature scenes in which characters deliver sermons of such length and verbal dexterity that for a time, the text exchanges the formal parameters of fiction for those of oral literature. Given the many other echoes of Ellison in Morrison&rsquo;s novels, both in structure and prose style, <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">these scenes suggest Ellison&rsquo;s direct influence on Morrison.</span></p>\n<p>Text 2</p>\n<p>In their destabilizing effect on literary form, the sermons in Morrison&rsquo;s works recall those in Ellison&rsquo;s. Yet literature by Black Americans abounds in moments where interpolated speech erodes the division between oral and written forms that literature in English has traditionally observed. Morrison&rsquo;s use of the sermon is attributable not only to the influence of Ellison but also to a community-wide strategy of resistance to externally imposed literary conventions.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely characterize the underlined claim in Text 1?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8fa1c8bd-cdea-4eff-802e-53b786079cbb","content":"<p>As failing to consider Ellison&rsquo;s and Morrison&rsquo;s equivalent uses of the sermon within the wider cultural context in which they wrote</p>"},{"id":"fb4a5e70-7426-433d-a640-65b1f91b1147","content":"<p>As misunderstanding the function of sermons in novels by Black American writers other than Ellison and Morrison</p>"},{"id":"85dfbf48-16ce-4e6c-9128-1d7c69f21d43","content":"<p>As disregarding points of structural and stylistic divergence between the works of Ellison and those of Morrison</p>"},{"id":"610d33c5-5dbb-48c7-bf86-338d61beba4f","content":"<p>As being indebted to the tradition of resisting literary conventions that privilege written forms, such as novels, over sermons and other oral forms</p>"}],"keys":["8fa1c8bd-cdea-4eff-802e-53b786079cbb"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The author of Text 2 argues that Morrison&rsquo;s use of the sermon is not only influenced by Ellison, but also by a &ldquo;community-wide strategy of resistance&rdquo; to literary conventions practiced by Black American authors. Ellison, Text 2 alleges, is only one of many influences on Morrison. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Neither text specifically mentions sermons in works by authors other than Morrison or Ellison, only a tendency towards eroding &ldquo;the division between oral and written forms&rdquo; among Black American writers. Choice C is incorrect. Both texts describe similarities between the works of Ellison and Morrison, and neither points out instances of divergence. Text 2 simply suggests that Morrison was influenced by more than just Ellison. Choice D is incorrect. While Text 2 does discuss Morrison&rsquo;s resistance to certain literary conventions, it&rsquo;s unclear what it would mean for the underlined claim to be &ldquo;indebted&rdquo; to that tradition. This choice recycles language from the text, but not in a way that makes any coherent point. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-CTC-H005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"779a3333-321f-4696-8583-c37d9735704b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959365,"updateDate":1691007959365}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959365, 1691007959365),
    ($SATQ$e4f312c5$SATQ$, $SATQ$2d3316e7-0698-4c60-bf30-9fbd363c2a15$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$fa7b8d57-4b45-4fe8-bf6e-6c6b8263b05f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While most animals are incapable of passing somatic mutations&mdash;genetic alterations that arise in an organism&rsquo;s nonreproductive cells&mdash;on to their offspring, elkhorn coral (<em>Acropora palmata</em>) presents an intriguing <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: in a 2022 study, researchers found that elkhorn coral produced offspring that inherited somatic mutations from a parent.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bfe9208b-95e2-4a44-8c6c-9d8b5cff8e08","content":"<p>hypothesis</p>"},{"id":"f4c0b577-a1b2-42ae-8a5b-ea7d6e7266b1","content":"<p>affinity</p>"},{"id":"d5b311ce-a8d1-4690-9b07-7246e1fee211","content":"<p>anomaly</p>"},{"id":"eaa92ae7-a9fd-4fd9-af0f-5a81887d8231","content":"<p>corroboration</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d5b311ce-a8d1-4690-9b07-7246e1fee211"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. An "anomaly" is something that deviates from norms or expectations. In this case, the elkhorn coral is an anomaly because it can pass on somatic mutations, whereas most other animals can&rsquo;t.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. A "hypothesis" is "a theory about something," but no theories are provided about elkhorn coral in this text, just facts. Choice B is incorrect. "Affinity" represents "an inclination or liking toward something." As genetic mutations tend to occur without any conscious effort, you can&rsquo;t really have an inclination toward passing on somatic mutations. Choice D is incorrect. "Corroboration" means "evidence to support or prove something." Because elkhorn coral do the opposite of what most animals do, they do not provide corroboration of the theory that somatic mutations can&rsquo;t be passed onto offspring. Rather, they show the opposite.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$63c1b4d3-f35d-434b-a259-be297d7da532$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e4f312c5","external_id":"2d3316e7-0698-4c60-bf30-9fbd363c2a15","vaultid":"fa7b8d57-4b45-4fe8-bf6e-6c6b8263b05f","uId":"a8f762cd-8747-4afe-b999-1949fae8c235","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While most animals are incapable of passing somatic mutations&mdash;genetic alterations that arise in an organism&rsquo;s nonreproductive cells&mdash;on to their offspring, elkhorn coral (<em>Acropora palmata</em>) presents an intriguing <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: in a 2022 study, researchers found that elkhorn coral produced offspring that inherited somatic mutations from a parent.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bfe9208b-95e2-4a44-8c6c-9d8b5cff8e08","content":"<p>hypothesis</p>"},{"id":"f4c0b577-a1b2-42ae-8a5b-ea7d6e7266b1","content":"<p>affinity</p>"},{"id":"d5b311ce-a8d1-4690-9b07-7246e1fee211","content":"<p>anomaly</p>"},{"id":"eaa92ae7-a9fd-4fd9-af0f-5a81887d8231","content":"<p>corroboration</p>"}],"keys":["d5b311ce-a8d1-4690-9b07-7246e1fee211"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. An \"anomaly\" is something that deviates from norms or expectations. In this case, the elkhorn coral is an anomaly because it can pass on somatic mutations, whereas most other animals can&rsquo;t.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. A \"hypothesis\" is \"a theory about something,\" but no theories are provided about elkhorn coral in this text, just facts. Choice B is incorrect. \"Affinity\" represents \"an inclination or liking toward something.\" As genetic mutations tend to occur without any conscious effort, you can&rsquo;t really have an inclination toward passing on somatic mutations. Choice D is incorrect. \"Corroboration\" means \"evidence to support or prove something.\" Because elkhorn coral do the opposite of what most animals do, they do not provide corroboration of the theory that somatic mutations can&rsquo;t be passed onto offspring. Rather, they show the opposite.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"63c1b4d3-f35d-434b-a259-be297d7da532","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$e56b66e5$SATQ$, $SATQ$13e8c84b-e1c2-43b6-a7a2-bb284fb43c68$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$058ae5cd-0dcb-45a2-82d9-4e6983e11373$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Set in a world where science fiction tropes exist as everyday realities, Charles Yu&rsquo;s 2010 novel <em>How to Live Safely in a Science Fictional Universe</em> traces a time traveler&rsquo;s quest to find his father. Because the journey at the novel&rsquo;s center is so <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>, with the protagonist ricocheting chaotically across time, the reader often wonders whether the pair will ever be reunited.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b97984d3-ab63-4f9c-8f9d-fbc567d98c89","content":"<p>haphazard</p>"},{"id":"4c1ed494-dffc-4bd8-8e06-f737717fc1c4","content":"<p>premeditated</p>"},{"id":"2dadd24f-c2f5-4cad-89fd-c45e0d8f72b4","content":"<p>inspirational</p>"},{"id":"cf5e7e14-6c38-4779-8379-e508b4502db5","content":"<p>fruitless</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b97984d3-ab63-4f9c-8f9d-fbc567d98c89"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Yu&rsquo;s novel. In this context, &ldquo;haphazard&rdquo; means marked by a lack of plan or order. The text indicates that the quest featured in the novel, which involves the protagonist bouncing across time, is chaotic and causes the reader to often wonder what will happen. This context suggests that the protagonist&rsquo;s journey seems to be marked by a lack of order. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the journey featured in Yu&rsquo;s novel involves a character &ldquo;ricocheting chaotically,&rdquo; or bouncing in a disordered way, across time and causes the reader to often wonder what will happen. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a chaotic journey seems &ldquo;premeditated,&rdquo; or characterized by forethought and planning. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t give any indication that readers regard the journey in Yu&rsquo;s novel as &ldquo;inspirational,&rdquo; or as causing extraordinarily creative or brilliant thoughts or actions; instead, the text focuses on the idea that the protagonist&rsquo;s journey is chaotic, or disordered, and doesn&rsquo;t give readers a clear sense of what will happen. Choice D is incorrect. Rather than suggesting that the journey featured in Yu&rsquo;s novel is &ldquo;fruitless,&rdquo; or has an unsuccessful outcome, the text focuses on the idea that while reading about the protagonist&rsquo;s chaotic movements across time, readers are often unsure of what will happen&mdash;that is, they don&rsquo;t know whether the protagonist will be successful in finding his father. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-976-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e7c53bd0-038b-4edc-a09b-4d422b9fa04a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e56b66e5","external_id":"13e8c84b-e1c2-43b6-a7a2-bb284fb43c68","vaultid":"058ae5cd-0dcb-45a2-82d9-4e6983e11373","uId":"c9cd45f4-7fbe-44ef-8120-5b5846f2f601","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Set in a world where science fiction tropes exist as everyday realities, Charles Yu&rsquo;s 2010 novel <em>How to Live Safely in a Science Fictional Universe</em> traces a time traveler&rsquo;s quest to find his father. Because the journey at the novel&rsquo;s center is so <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>, with the protagonist ricocheting chaotically across time, the reader often wonders whether the pair will ever be reunited.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b97984d3-ab63-4f9c-8f9d-fbc567d98c89","content":"<p>haphazard</p>"},{"id":"4c1ed494-dffc-4bd8-8e06-f737717fc1c4","content":"<p>premeditated</p>"},{"id":"2dadd24f-c2f5-4cad-89fd-c45e0d8f72b4","content":"<p>inspirational</p>"},{"id":"cf5e7e14-6c38-4779-8379-e508b4502db5","content":"<p>fruitless</p>"}],"keys":["b97984d3-ab63-4f9c-8f9d-fbc567d98c89"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Yu&rsquo;s novel. In this context, &ldquo;haphazard&rdquo; means marked by a lack of plan or order. The text indicates that the quest featured in the novel, which involves the protagonist bouncing across time, is chaotic and causes the reader to often wonder what will happen. This context suggests that the protagonist&rsquo;s journey seems to be marked by a lack of order. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text indicates that the journey featured in Yu&rsquo;s novel involves a character &ldquo;ricocheting chaotically,&rdquo; or bouncing in a disordered way, across time and causes the reader to often wonder what will happen. It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a chaotic journey seems &ldquo;premeditated,&rdquo; or characterized by forethought and planning. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t give any indication that readers regard the journey in Yu&rsquo;s novel as &ldquo;inspirational,&rdquo; or as causing extraordinarily creative or brilliant thoughts or actions; instead, the text focuses on the idea that the protagonist&rsquo;s journey is chaotic, or disordered, and doesn&rsquo;t give readers a clear sense of what will happen. Choice D is incorrect. Rather than suggesting that the journey featured in Yu&rsquo;s novel is &ldquo;fruitless,&rdquo; or has an unsuccessful outcome, the text focuses on the idea that while reading about the protagonist&rsquo;s chaotic movements across time, readers are often unsure of what will happen&mdash;that is, they don&rsquo;t know whether the protagonist will be successful in finding his father. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-976-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e7c53bd0-038b-4edc-a09b-4d422b9fa04a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959357,"updateDate":1691007959357}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959357, 1691007959357),
    ($SATQ$e5ca7687$SATQ$, $SATQ$85e286ca-e741-446a-a2f4-e98e6095a4dc$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Archaeologists studying the ancient city of Pompeii in Italy recently discovered a well-preserved food shop known as a <span lang="la"><em>thermopolium</em></span>. The site contains food remains, artworks, and decorations. These items give researchers a better understanding of what daily life in Pompeii may have been like. For example, the archaeologists found a ceramic jar that they believe likely contained a meat and seafood stew.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a98984ca-8951-4886-b3cc-4430f5a84bf0","content":"<p>To compare ancient artworks with modern ones</p>"},{"id":"31ed447e-f7da-4867-b767-9f91bf57ad59","content":"<p>To discuss the political system of Italy</p>"},{"id":"f49544b0-2e6a-4c62-ae07-7f6ac7845651","content":"<p>To present a recent archaeological discovery&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"63553a65-9d42-46f1-b061-0fa0f35e2899","content":"<p>To describe a region&rsquo;s climate</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f49544b0-2e6a-4c62-ae07-7f6ac7845651"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The text states that archaeologists recently discovered a well-preserved food shop, or <span lang="la"><em>thermopolium</em></span>, in Pompeii, Italy.  The text then further describes the contents of the discovery and provides an example of what was found. Thus, the overall purpose of the text is to present a recent archaeological discovery.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text states that archaeologists found ancient artworks, it doesn&rsquo;t compare these artworks to modern ones or to any other artworks. Choice B is incorrect. Although the archaeological discovery discussed in the text was made in Italy, the text doesn&rsquo;t provide any information about politics or government in Italy. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the climate where the archaeological discovery was made or in any other region.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e5ca7687","external_id":"85e286ca-e741-446a-a2f4-e98e6095a4dc","vaultid":null,"uId":"7e5bce0a-8e6c-416d-a05a-7f82981e35b9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Archaeologists studying the ancient city of Pompeii in Italy recently discovered a well-preserved food shop known as a <span lang=\"la\"><em>thermopolium</em></span>. The site contains food remains, artworks, and decorations. These items give researchers a better understanding of what daily life in Pompeii may have been like. For example, the archaeologists found a ceramic jar that they believe likely contained a meat and seafood stew.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a98984ca-8951-4886-b3cc-4430f5a84bf0","content":"<p>To compare ancient artworks with modern ones</p>"},{"id":"31ed447e-f7da-4867-b767-9f91bf57ad59","content":"<p>To discuss the political system of Italy</p>"},{"id":"f49544b0-2e6a-4c62-ae07-7f6ac7845651","content":"<p>To present a recent archaeological discovery&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"63553a65-9d42-46f1-b061-0fa0f35e2899","content":"<p>To describe a region&rsquo;s climate</p>"}],"keys":["f49544b0-2e6a-4c62-ae07-7f6ac7845651"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text. The text states that archaeologists recently discovered a well-preserved food shop, or <span lang=\"la\"><em>thermopolium</em></span>, in Pompeii, Italy.  The text then further describes the contents of the discovery and provides an example of what was found. Thus, the overall purpose of the text is to present a recent archaeological discovery.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text states that archaeologists found ancient artworks, it doesn&rsquo;t compare these artworks to modern ones or to any other artworks. Choice B is incorrect. Although the archaeological discovery discussed in the text was made in Italy, the text doesn&rsquo;t provide any information about politics or government in Italy. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the climate where the archaeological discovery was made or in any other region.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669210,"updateDate":1743430669210}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669210, 1743430669210),
    ($SATQ$e5da61f1$SATQ$, $SATQ$9151070e-2d5c-4763-9c7f-e3a2018d7c53$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$84c607ba-c2b2-4d32-b2d4-a63a3d8752b4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Charles Chesnutt&rsquo;s 1899 short story &ldquo;Mars Jeems&rsquo;s Nightmare.&rdquo; The narrator and his wife have recently moved to the southern United States, and Julius is their carriage driver.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Julius [was] very useful when we moved to our new residence. He had a thorough knowledge of the neighborhood, was familiar with the roads and the watercourses, knew the qualities of the various soils and what they would produce, and where the best hunting and fishing were to be had. He was a marvelous hand in the management of horses and dogs.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"37c70264-5d22-474b-9367-f89e50d164d3","content":"<p>To compare the narrator&rsquo;s reaction to a new home with his wife&rsquo;s reaction</p>"},{"id":"5a7008ef-39b0-4330-8b9b-c46afd37f37e","content":"<p>To give an example of Julius&rsquo;s knowledge about soil</p>"},{"id":"9c1c2ca3-4713-4fbb-bc6e-677438945e66","content":"<p>To show that the narrator and Julius often hunt and fish together</p>"},{"id":"8f6618b7-12d9-4e78-8b7a-d34833b1a211","content":"<p>To explain different ways in which Julius was helpful</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8f6618b7-12d9-4e78-8b7a-d34833b1a211"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text, which is to explain different ways in which Julius was helpful. The text begins with the narrator stating that Julius was very helpful to him and his wife when they moved to their new residence. The narrator then provides a list of examples to illustrate Julius&rsquo;s helpfulness. For instance, the narrator states that Julius was familiar with the neighborhood&rsquo;s roads, which suggests that he was helpful in navigating them, and that Julius helped manage the horses and dogs. The text&rsquo;s many examples of Julius&rsquo;s usefulness reinforce just how helpful he was and in how many different ways. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t portray either the narrator&rsquo;s or his wife&rsquo;s reaction to their new home. Rather, the text focuses on how Julius was useful to the narrator and his wife in their new home. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text states that Julius was knowledgeable about the soil, this is one of several supporting details that illustrate how helpful Julius was. Moreover, the text merely states that Julius was knowledgeable about soil; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of that knowledge. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest the frequency with which the narrator and Julius hunted and fished together. In fact, it&rsquo;s unclear from the text whether the narrator and Julius hunted and fished together at all. The text merely indicates that Julius knew the best places to hunt and fish&mdash;a detail that supports the text&rsquo;s main purpose by conveying Julius&rsquo;s usefulness. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-045-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$322d08c5-ab8f-4603-a9fe-5318905dc247$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e5da61f1","external_id":"9151070e-2d5c-4763-9c7f-e3a2018d7c53","vaultid":"84c607ba-c2b2-4d32-b2d4-a63a3d8752b4","uId":"a11efda3-36f3-406a-a3a7-dc51a1f9dcba","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Charles Chesnutt&rsquo;s 1899 short story &ldquo;Mars Jeems&rsquo;s Nightmare.&rdquo; The narrator and his wife have recently moved to the southern United States, and Julius is their carriage driver.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Julius [was] very useful when we moved to our new residence. He had a thorough knowledge of the neighborhood, was familiar with the roads and the watercourses, knew the qualities of the various soils and what they would produce, and where the best hunting and fishing were to be had. He was a marvelous hand in the management of horses and dogs.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"37c70264-5d22-474b-9367-f89e50d164d3","content":"<p>To compare the narrator&rsquo;s reaction to a new home with his wife&rsquo;s reaction</p>"},{"id":"5a7008ef-39b0-4330-8b9b-c46afd37f37e","content":"<p>To give an example of Julius&rsquo;s knowledge about soil</p>"},{"id":"9c1c2ca3-4713-4fbb-bc6e-677438945e66","content":"<p>To show that the narrator and Julius often hunt and fish together</p>"},{"id":"8f6618b7-12d9-4e78-8b7a-d34833b1a211","content":"<p>To explain different ways in which Julius was helpful</p>"}],"keys":["8f6618b7-12d9-4e78-8b7a-d34833b1a211"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the main purpose of the text, which is to explain different ways in which Julius was helpful. The text begins with the narrator stating that Julius was very helpful to him and his wife when they moved to their new residence. The narrator then provides a list of examples to illustrate Julius&rsquo;s helpfulness. For instance, the narrator states that Julius was familiar with the neighborhood&rsquo;s roads, which suggests that he was helpful in navigating them, and that Julius helped manage the horses and dogs. The text&rsquo;s many examples of Julius&rsquo;s usefulness reinforce just how helpful he was and in how many different ways. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t portray either the narrator&rsquo;s or his wife&rsquo;s reaction to their new home. Rather, the text focuses on how Julius was useful to the narrator and his wife in their new home. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text states that Julius was knowledgeable about the soil, this is one of several supporting details that illustrate how helpful Julius was. Moreover, the text merely states that Julius was knowledgeable about soil; it doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of that knowledge. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s nothing in the text to suggest the frequency with which the narrator and Julius hunted and fished together. In fact, it&rsquo;s unclear from the text whether the narrator and Julius hunted and fished together at all. The text merely indicates that Julius knew the best places to hunt and fish&mdash;a detail that supports the text&rsquo;s main purpose by conveying Julius&rsquo;s usefulness. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-045-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"322d08c5-ab8f-4603-a9fe-5318905dc247","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959323,"updateDate":1691007959323}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959323, 1691007959323)
